// 六级 8000 词 · 每组配套短文 freq.cet6（由 build/passages/freq.cet6.txt 生成）
CET6.passages("freq.cet6",{"1":{"w":["participant","environmental","cognitive","productivity","emission","trait","interact","assert","psychology","innovation","foster","bias","toxic","sector","context","strive","peer","intervention","marathon","intervene"],"title":"Green Offices and the Working Mind","t":"Can plants make us smarter at work? A growing body of research in **psychology** suggests that they can. In one recent study, each **participant** spent a week in either an ordinary office or one filled with plants and natural light. Those in the green offices performed better on **cognitive** tests and reported feeling calmer.\n\nThe findings matter because office workers spend most of their waking hours indoors. Poor air quality, including **toxic** chemicals released by cheap furniture and paint, can reduce concentration. Researchers **assert** that improving the **environmental** quality of offices is one of the cheapest ways to raise **productivity**. Some companies in the technology **sector** have already redesigned their workplaces, cutting energy use and carbon **emissions|emission** at the same time.\n\nPersonality also plays a role. Not everyone responds to the same environment in the same way, and a person's **traits|trait**, such as openness or anxiety, shape how they **interact** with colleagues. Leaders who want to **foster** **innovation** should therefore give employees some control over their surroundings. In this **context**, a simple change like allowing people to choose where to sit can make a real difference.\n\nExperts warn, however, against **bias** in how such studies are reported. Many are funded by companies that sell office plants, and their results may be exaggerated. Careful scientists **strive** to test their ideas in different countries and workplaces, and to have their work checked by a **peer** before publication.\n\nImproving the workplace is a **marathon**, not a sprint. No single **intervention** will solve every problem, and managers should not **intervene** too heavily in how employees organise their desks. But the evidence suggests that a little green can go a long way."},"2":{"w":["currently","alleviate","capability","chronic","aesthetic","perception","plateau","immune","correlation","diminish","nutrition","elite","corporate","infrastructure","underlying","client","web","recycle","concede","facilitate"],"title":"Why Cities Need Parks","t":"City planners have long treated parks as a luxury, something to add once the roads and railways are built. But that view is changing. **Currently**, a number of studies show a strong **correlation** between access to green space and better health, and many experts now argue that parks are as essential as any other part of urban **infrastructure**.\n\nThe benefits are wide-ranging. Regular walks in a park can **alleviate** stress and lower the risk of **chronic** diseases such as heart disease and diabetes. Some research suggests that contact with nature may even strengthen the **immune** system. Children who play outdoors tend to have better **nutrition** habits and higher levels of physical **capability**.\n\nParks also shape our **perception** of the city itself. A well-designed park has **aesthetic** value: it makes a neighbourhood feel safer and more pleasant. Property developers are aware of this, and **corporate** investors increasingly pay to create green spaces near their buildings to attract **clients|client** and tenants.\n\nCritics **concede** that parks bring benefits, but they worry about who enjoys them. In many cities, the best parks are located in wealthy areas, used mainly by an **elite** who already live healthy lives. The **underlying** problem, they argue, is inequality, not a lack of trees.\n\nPlanners are trying to respond. Some cities now **recycle** abandoned railway lines into long, narrow parks that connect poor and rich neighbourhoods like a **web**. Others use simple designs to **facilitate** community gardening. Health improvements often reach a **plateau** after the first few years, researchers note, but the social benefits do not **diminish**. A good park, it seems, keeps giving."},"3":{"w":["spectrum","entrepreneur","appealing","correlate","extinction","funding","underestimate","dominant","automate","makeup","solidarity","engagement","incentive","confront","accessible","humanity","incorporate","transition","vulnerable","adverse"],"title":"Business Against Extinction","t":"Every year, thousands of species move closer to **extinction**, and governments struggle to find the **funding** to protect them. Increasingly, a new kind of **entrepreneur** is stepping in. These business leaders argue that saving nature can be both profitable and **appealing** to customers.\n\nTheir methods cover a wide **spectrum**. Some start companies that sell coffee grown in forests without cutting down trees. Others use drones and software to **automate** the monitoring of wildlife, making conservation cheaper and more **accessible** to small communities. One firm has found ways to **incorporate** recycled plastic from the ocean into shoes and bags.\n\nSupporters say these businesses offer a powerful **incentive**: when local people can earn money by protecting animals, they are less likely to hunt them. Research suggests that income from tourism and sustainable farming tends to **correlate** with healthier wildlife populations. Too often, experts say, we **underestimate** the role of local **engagement** in conservation.\n\nYet critics urge caution. When profit becomes the **dominant** goal, companies may choose to protect only the most attractive species, such as pandas or tigers, while ignoring less popular insects and plants. Some projects have also had **adverse** effects on poor farmers, who lost land to new nature reserves. Business, they argue, cannot replace government.\n\nBoth sides agree that the world faces a difficult **transition**. Protecting **vulnerable** species will require us to **confront** hard questions about growth and consumption. The genetic **makeup** of life on Earth took millions of years to develop, and once lost, it cannot be recovered. Success will depend on **solidarity** between businesses, governments and ordinary people, and on recognising that the fate of nature is also the fate of **humanity**."},"4":{"w":["preservation","consistently","intricate","literally","prevalent","promotion","combat","deteriorate","landscape","positively","update","discourse","sensor","stray","hemisphere","fulfill","refrain","deem","popularity","seemingly"],"title":"Saving the Painted Caves","t":"High in the desert of northwest China, a group of ancient caves holds some of the most **intricate** wall paintings in the world. For more than a thousand years, monks and artists covered the walls with images of gods, music and daily life. Today, these paintings face a serious threat, and their **preservation** has become a race against time.\n\nThe main danger is change in the environment. The paintings **deteriorate** quickly when humidity rises, and the breath of thousands of tourists **literally** changes the air inside the caves. As tourism grows in **popularity**, the problem becomes more **prevalent**. To **combat** it, scientists have installed a **sensor** in each cave to measure temperature, moisture and carbon dioxide. When the levels become dangerous, the cave is closed.\n\nThe approach seems to be working. Data show that conditions have improved **consistently** since the system was introduced, and visitors have responded **positively** to new rules. Managers now **update** the number of tourists allowed each day, and they **refrain** from opening the most fragile caves at all. A **seemingly** small change, such as shortening each visit by five minutes, can protect the walls for decades.\n\nThe project has also changed public **discourse** about heritage. In the past, the **promotion** of tourism was **deemed|deem** more important than protection. Now many experts argue that a site's true value lies in passing it on to future generations. Visitors can explore the caves online through detailed digital copies, and some experts believe this will **fulfill** most people's curiosity.\n\nSimilar sites in the southern **hemisphere** have adopted the same methods. Even the **stray** cats that once wandered through the site have been moved to a nearby shelter. Across the ancient **landscape**, the message is clear: some treasures must be visited gently, or not at all."},"5":{"w":["specifically","depict","endeavor","foresee","irritate","meaningful","skeptical","constraint","evaluation","layman","nutrient","optimism","caution","spouse","constrain","ethical","designate","timely","unprecedented","assessment"],"title":"Can an App Tell You What to Eat?","t":"Diet apps have become hugely popular. With a few taps, users can record their meals, count calories and receive advice on every **nutrient** they need. Supporters see an **unprecedented** opportunity to improve public health. But many doctors remain **skeptical**.\n\nThe apps promise a lot. Some claim to **foresee** health problems before they appear, using data about weight, sleep and exercise. Others **depict** each meal as a coloured chart, making nutrition easy for any **layman** to understand. In a recent **evaluation** of twenty popular apps, researchers found that users lost weight more quickly than people who received no help.\n\nHowever, the study also raised concerns. Few apps were designed **specifically** for people with medical conditions, and some gave advice that could be dangerous. \"An app cannot replace a proper **assessment** by a doctor,\" one researcher warned. Users should treat the advice with **caution**, especially if they are pregnant or taking medicine.\n\nThere are **ethical** questions too. Many apps collect personal data and sell it to advertisers. Some send so many messages that they **irritate** users or make them anxious about food. Experts argue that clear rules are needed to **constrain** how companies use health data, and that governments should **designate** an independent body to check the apps' claims. Without such a **constraint**, trust may disappear.\n\nDespite these problems, there is reason for **optimism**. Used wisely, an app can provide **timely** reminders and help people make **meaningful** changes to their habits. The best results come when users share their goals with a **spouse** or friend. In the end, healthy eating remains a human **endeavor**; technology can support it, but it cannot do the work for us."},"6":{"w":["dedicate","obsession","subtle","susceptible","distract","norm","producer","ascertain","chip","diagnose","literacy","profound","cape","consensus","forge","hinder","mechanism","realistic","accomplishment","appreciation"],"title":"Screens and the Young Reader","t":"Teachers around the world have noticed a **subtle** but worrying change. Children who once read books for hours now struggle to finish a single chapter. Many experts believe that the culprit is the smartphone, and that young minds are especially **susceptible** to its pull.\n\nThe **mechanism** is not hard to understand. Apps are designed to **distract** us, rewarding every tap with a new image or message. Each phone contains a powerful **chip** that can deliver endless content, and the **producer** of that content profits from our attention. For some teenagers, checking the phone has become an **obsession**, and it can seriously **hinder** their ability to concentrate on long texts.\n\nResearchers have tried to **ascertain** how serious the problem is. It is difficult to **diagnose** a \"reading problem\" in the same way as a medical condition, but tests show that deep reading skills are declining in many countries. There is now a broad **consensus** that strong **literacy** requires daily practice with books, not just short messages.\n\nThe consequences may be **profound**. Reading long texts teaches patience, empathy and an **appreciation** of complex ideas. Some educators fear that a generation raised on screens will see short attention spans as the new **norm**.\n\nYet there are reasons for hope. A school on a remote **cape** in Norway has banned phones during the day and asked teachers to **dedicate** the first twenty minutes of every lesson to silent reading. Within a year, scores rose sharply, and students described finishing a novel as a real **accomplishment**. The school's head is **realistic**: phones will not disappear. But she believes schools can help children **forge** a healthier relationship with technology, one page at a time."},"7":{"w":["catastrophe","cite","initiate","convincing","cynical","ecosystem","exploration","historian","patent","profile","trigger","fitness","oak","bamboo","patron","snob","broaden","enrich","excel","nurture"],"title":"The Return of the Ancient Forest","t":"Two hundred years ago, the hills outside the town were covered by a great **oak** forest. By 1900, almost every tree had been cut down for farmland and ships. Now a bold project is trying to bring it back, and its supporters believe it could **enrich** the whole region.\n\nThe idea came from a local **historian** who spent years studying old maps and letters. In her research, she would often **cite** records showing that the forest once supported wolves, deer and hundreds of bird species. She argued that losing the forest had been an ecological **catastrophe**, and that restoring it would rebuild an entire **ecosystem**.\n\nAt first, many residents were **cynical**. They thought the plan was the dream of a wealthy **patron**, a city **snob** who wanted a beautiful view. But the historian's evidence was **convincing**, and the council agreed to **initiate** a trial. Volunteers planted ten thousand young trees in the first year. In wetter areas, they also planted fast-growing **bamboo** to protect the soil.\n\nThe project has done more than plant trees. Local schools use the forest for science lessons and nature **exploration**, helping to **broaden** children's understanding of the environment. Doctors report that walking in the woods has improved residents' **fitness**. One young inventor even took out a **patent** on a simple device that protects young trees from deer.\n\nExperts warn that a single dry summer could **trigger** fires and destroy years of work. But the forest is growing. The project's **profile** has risen nationally, and other towns have asked for advice. The historian compares it to the oak itself. \"Some trees **excel** at growing quickly; oaks excel at lasting,\" she says. \"We must **nurture** this forest patiently. Our grandchildren will enjoy the shade.\""},"8":{"w":["upgrade","absurd","aggravate","controversial","conviction","disrupt","elevate","fascinate","legacy","legitimate","causal","compensation","counterpart","deprive","donate","economically","fossil","integrity","random","shabby"],"title":"Who Owns a Fossil?","t":"When a farmer in Montana found a dinosaur **fossil** on his land, he had no idea it would lead to a legal battle lasting ten years. Scientists described the skeleton as one of the most complete ever found, and its value was estimated at millions of dollars. The question was simple but **controversial**: who should own it?\n\nThe farmer believed he had a **legitimate** claim, since the bones were found on his property. Museums argued that such discoveries belong to the public and should not be sold to the highest bidder. Some scientists found it **absurd** that a priceless part of Earth's history could end up in a private collection, where it might **disrupt** research for decades.\n\nThe case raised difficult questions about **integrity** in science. Fossil hunters sometimes dig carelessly to find valuable bones quickly, which can **aggravate** damage to the surrounding rocks and **deprive** researchers of important information. A scientist needs to know not only what a fossil is, but where and how it was found, to understand any **causal** link between climate change and extinction.\n\nIn the end, a court ruled in the farmer's favour. However, the story had a surprising ending. Instead of selling the skeleton abroad, the farmer chose to **donate** it to a local museum in exchange for modest **compensation**. \"I have a strong **conviction** that children should be able to see it,\" he said. The museum, once **shabby** and almost empty, has been renovated and now attracts thousands of visitors.\n\nThe skeleton continues to **fascinate** the public, and the decision has helped to **elevate** the town's reputation. It has also helped the town **economically**: visitors spend money in local shops. Scientists have even been able to **upgrade** their research with new scanning equipment. Unlike his **counterpart** in a similar case, who sold his fossil to a **random** collector, the farmer has left a lasting **legacy**."},"9":{"w":["signify","symptom","cluster","ethics","mobility","prey","ritual","eclipse","erosion","millionaire","perfection","persistence","instrumental","adolescent","supervision","allocate","anonymous","discrepancy","domain","endanger"],"title":"Growing Up Online","t":"Adolescence has always been a difficult time, but today's teenagers face new pressures. Psychologists report a **cluster** of worrying trends among young people: rising anxiety, poor sleep and a constant need for approval. Many believe that social media plays an **instrumental** role.\n\nFor a typical **adolescent**, checking social media has become a daily **ritual**. Every like or comment can **signify** acceptance or rejection. Images of perfect bodies and the luxurious lives of a young **millionaire** create unrealistic standards, and the pursuit of **perfection** can lead to a slow **erosion** of self-esteem. Low mood, loss of appetite and difficulty sleeping are a common **symptom** of this pressure.\n\nSome dangers are more serious. **Anonymous** accounts make it easy to bully others without consequences, and young users can fall **prey** to online criminals. Researchers have also found a **discrepancy** between what parents believe their children see online and what they actually see. This lack of **supervision** may **endanger** young people.\n\nThese problems raise important questions of **ethics**. Should technology companies be responsible for the effects of their products? Many experts think so. They argue that governments should **allocate** more money to research and require companies to design safer platforms. Some countries now limit the **mobility** of young users across certain apps, requiring age checks.\n\nNot everything about social media is negative. It can connect isolated teenagers with supportive communities and help them explore a new **domain** of interests. With **persistence**, parents and schools can teach young people to use it wisely. The goal, one psychologist says, is not to let the online world **eclipse** real life, but to keep it in its proper place."},"10":{"w":["epidemic","exotic","expertise","flaw","imperative","physically","sentiment","setback","unemployment","ambitious","baffle","collaboration","colonial","competitor","disperse","evolutionary","formidable","inhibit","longevity","optimal"],"title":"The Secrets of a Long Life","t":"The naked mole rat is not a beautiful animal. It is small, pink and almost blind, and it lives in underground tunnels in East Africa. Yet scientists are deeply interested in it, because it can live for more than thirty years, far longer than any other rodent of its size. Its **longevity** continues to **baffle** biologists.\n\nPart of the answer may be **evolutionary**. Mole rats are **colonial** animals: they live in large groups, much like bees, with a single queen. Life underground protects them from predators, and their bodies seem to **inhibit** the growth of cancer cells. Even when they are old, they remain **physically** strong. Their way of life gives them a **formidable** advantage over their **competitor** species above ground.\n\nUnderstanding these animals is now an **imperative** for researchers who study human ageing. An **ambitious** international **collaboration** has brought together scientists with **expertise** in genetics, medicine and zoology. Their goal is to identify the **optimal** conditions for healthy ageing. If they succeed, their work might one day help to prevent the **epidemic** of age-related diseases in developed countries.\n\nProgress has not been easy. The project suffered a serious **setback** when a key experiment was found to contain a **flaw**, and funding cuts have led to **unemployment** among young researchers. Some members of the public question whether money should be spent on such an **exotic** creature.\n\nStill, public **sentiment** has slowly changed. Videos of mole rats have gone viral online, and scientists now regularly **disperse** their findings through short films and public talks. As one researcher put it, \"The ugliest animal on Earth may hold the key to a longer, healthier life for all of us.\""},"11":{"w":["portray","attendance","category","clinical","commute","curriculum","evenly","hypothesis","inspiration","overwhelm","persevere","prediction","presume","rigorous","romance","shorten","silicon","migrate","scarcity","sensation"],"title":"Leaving the Big City","t":"For decades, young people dreamed of moving to big cities. Novels and films would **portray** city life as a **romance** of opportunity and excitement. Today, however, a growing number of workers are choosing to **migrate** in the opposite direction, from crowded cities to small towns.\n\nSeveral factors explain the trend. Housing in major cities has become extremely expensive, and the **scarcity** of affordable homes forces many families into tiny flats. A long daily **commute** can **overwhelm** even the most energetic worker. When remote work became common, many people realised they could **shorten** their working day by hours simply by working from home. Even employees of **silicon** chip companies, once tied to their offices, now work from mountain villages.\n\nResearchers are testing a **hypothesis**: that moving to a quieter place improves mental health. Early results from **clinical** studies are promising. People who moved reported less stress and better sleep. But scientists warn against making a confident **prediction**. A **rigorous** study would need to follow families for many years, and we cannot simply **presume** that the benefits will last.\n\nSmall towns are changing as a result. Local schools report rising **attendance**, and some have expanded their **curriculum** to include coding and foreign languages. A new **category** of business has also appeared: coffee shops and shared offices for remote workers. In some villages, the arrival of city families has caused a **sensation**.\n\nNot every move succeeds. Some newcomers find rural life lonely, and jobs are not spread **evenly** across regions. Those who **persevere**, however, often describe their new life as a source of **inspiration**. \"I traded a small flat for a garden,\" one designer said. \"I have never been happier.\""},"12":{"w":["shark","paradox","postal","pregnant","surge","ample","endurance","enterprise","safeguard","surpass","tackle","arrogant","articulate","composer","comprehend","deficit","deploy","dignity","distinctive","distort"],"title":"The Truth About Sharks","t":"Few animals frighten people as much as the **shark**. Films and news reports often **distort** the truth, showing sharks as cruel killers. A famous **composer** once created music so frightening that, for many viewers, it still plays in their minds whenever they swim in the sea. Yet the real danger lies the other way round.\n\nHere lies a **paradox**: although sharks kill fewer than ten people a year, humans kill around a hundred million sharks. Many are caught for their fins, which are used in soup. Because sharks grow slowly and have few young, populations cannot recover quickly. When a **pregnant** female is caught, the loss is even greater. In some regions, numbers have fallen by more than ninety per cent.\n\nScientists are trying to **tackle** the problem. They **deploy** small satellite tags to follow sharks across oceans and study their remarkable **endurance**; some swim thousands of kilometres a year. One **articulate** marine biologist has become famous for explaining shark behaviour in simple terms, helping the public **comprehend** why these animals matter. Without sharks, she explains, smaller fish multiply and destroy coral reefs.\n\nSome countries have passed laws to **safeguard** sharks and ban the fin trade. Tourism is also helping. In recent years, there has been a **surge** in shark-diving holidays, and local **enterprise** has followed. In some islands, income from tourists now **surpasses|surpass** the money once earned from fishing, helping governments reduce a budget **deficit**. There is **ample** evidence that a living shark is worth far more than a dead one. One country even issued a **postal** stamp celebrating its shark population.\n\nExperts say it is **arrogant** to believe that humans can destroy a species that has survived for four hundred million years without consequences. Each shark, with its **distinctive** fin and quiet **dignity**, deserves our respect."},"13":{"w":["diversify","drastic","eccentric","entail","heighten","historic","impetus","indulge","inevitably","intriguing","permeate","plague","promptly","prone","resentment","striking","stun","turnover","verbal","versus"],"title":"A Mining Town Reinvents Itself","t":"For more than a century, the small town of Hollow Creek lived on coal. Then, twenty years ago, the mine closed. The change was **drastic**: almost half the population lost their jobs, and many young people left. **Inevitably**, shops shut down and houses stood empty.\n\nAt first, the mood was dark. **Resentment** against the government and the mining company began to **permeate** daily life, and people felt that the town had been abandoned. Poverty seemed to **plague** every street. Visitors were often **stunned|stun** by the number of boarded-up windows.\n\nThe **impetus** for change came from an unexpected source: an **eccentric** local artist who began painting colourful murals on the empty buildings. The results were **striking**. Photographs of the murals spread online, and tourists began to arrive. The town council responded **promptly**, offering small grants to anyone who wanted to open a café, gallery or guesthouse.\n\nRebuilding the economy has been hard work. Experts say a town that depends on one industry is **prone** to collapse, so Hollow Creek has tried to **diversify**. It now has a craft brewery, a music festival and a museum about its **historic** mine. Business **turnover** has grown steadily, and the population has stopped falling.\n\nThe transformation did **entail** some conflict. Older residents complained that the town was becoming a playground for wealthy visitors who come to **indulge** in expensive food and wine. Debates about \"tradition **versus** tourism\" sometimes became heated, with **verbal** arguments at council meetings. Yet most people agree that the changes have helped to **heighten** local pride. The town's story is an **intriguing** example of how communities can survive the end of an industry."},"14":{"w":["abundance","array","authentic","cater","commodity","complexity","destiny","dine","disturbance","earnings","ecology","erroneous","explicit","generalization","generalize","imitation","inclusive","infectious","intake","intent"],"title":"In Search of Authentic Food","t":"Food tourism has become one of the fastest-growing areas of travel. Visitors no longer simply want to see famous buildings; they want to **dine** where local people eat and taste dishes that feel **authentic**. Cities now promote an **array** of street markets, cooking classes and food tours.\n\nThe trend brings clear benefits. Restaurant **earnings** have risen in many historic neighbourhoods, and small farmers have found new markets. In a world of fast food and **imitation**, a traditional dish can become a valuable **commodity**. Some cities have even made food part of their **destiny**, building their whole tourism strategy around a single famous dish.\n\nBut food tourism also raises difficult questions. To **cater** to foreign visitors, some restaurants change their recipes, making them less spicy or adding sugar. Critics say this produces an **erroneous** picture of local culture. There are concerns about **ecology**, too: the **abundance** of tourists can create noise and waste, causing **disturbance** to residents.\n\nExperts warn against easy **generalization**. It is a mistake to **generalize** from one restaurant to a whole cuisine, and the **complexity** of any food culture cannot be captured in a single meal. Good food tours are **explicit** about what is traditional and what is new, and they try to be **inclusive**, sharing profits with local families.\n\nTravellers can help by eating thoughtfully. One food writer advises visitors to watch their **intake** of famous tourist dishes and to explore ordinary local cafés instead. \"Travel with the **intent** to learn, not just to eat,\" she says. Her enthusiasm is **infectious**, and her books have inspired thousands of readers to see food as a window into culture."},"15":{"w":["prerequisite","prescription","prestige","privacy","proficiency","similarity","speculate","stimulus","unpaid","vocal","analogy","mediate","nickname","nourish","pedestrian","thrifty","degradation","lawsuit","presentation","symmetry"],"title":"The Debate Over Unpaid Internships","t":"For many young people, an internship has become a **prerequisite** for a good job. Employers want graduates with practical experience, and a well-known company on a CV brings **prestige**. Yet many of these positions are **unpaid**, and the practice has become increasingly controversial.\n\nSupporters argue that internships **nourish** young talent. They provide a **stimulus** for learning, allowing students to develop **proficiency** in real work and to give a **presentation** to experienced professionals. Some compare internships to the training once offered by craftsmen to young workers, an **analogy** they believe shows the value of learning by doing.\n\nCritics are **vocal** in their opposition. Unpaid work, they argue, favours students from wealthy families, who can afford to live in expensive cities without a salary. Poorer students, however **thrifty**, cannot. This, they say, leads to a slow **degradation** of fairness in society. In several countries, former interns have filed a **lawsuit** against major companies, claiming they did real work without pay.\n\nThe **similarity** between internships and ordinary jobs is often striking. Some interns work long hours, manage customer accounts and even handle sensitive information that requires strict **privacy**. Governments have tried to **mediate** between employers and students, introducing rules that require pay for any intern who does productive work.\n\nOne student, given the **nickname** \"the free worker\" by his colleagues, summed up the problem. \"I wanted to learn,\" he said, \"but I also needed to eat.\" Advice such as \"just work harder\" is a **pedestrian** answer, he argued, not a real **prescription** for change.\n\nIt is hard to **speculate** about the future, but experts expect some form of payment to become normal. Ideally, they say, the system should have a kind of **symmetry**: students give their time and effort, and employers give fair pay and real training in return."},"16":{"w":["adjacent","alongside","apt","assault","clash","conception","cooperative","fragile","geographical","haunt","heritage","incidence","nightmare","pasture","penalty","recipient","situated","slack","snack","studio"],"title":"Cheese, Wolves and a Mountain Village","t":"High in the Alps, a small village is **situated** on a green slope **adjacent** to a national park. For centuries, families here have grazed their cows on mountain **pasture** in summer and made cheese by hand. This tradition is part of the region's cultural **heritage**, and it was nearly lost.\n\nThirty years ago, young people were leaving, and cheese prices were falling. The farmers responded by forming a **cooperative**. By working **alongside** each other, sharing machines and selling under one name, they cut costs and reached new markets. Today the cooperative's cheese is sold in cities across Europe, and it was recently the **recipient** of an international food award. Visitors can watch cheese being made in a glass **studio** and buy a cheese **snack** for the walk home.\n\nBut the village faces a new challenge. Wolves have returned to the mountains, and the **incidence** of attacks on sheep and young cattle has risen sharply. For some farmers, losing animals has become a recurring **nightmare**. They describe the wolves' return as an **assault** on their way of life, and memories of past losses continue to **haunt** them.\n\nThis has caused a **clash** between farmers and conservationists. Environmental groups argue that wolves are a vital part of the ecosystem and that killing them should bring a heavy **penalty**. Farmers reply that city dwellers have a romantic **conception** of nature and do not understand the realities of mountain life. One farmer put it **aptly|apt**: \"Everyone loves wolves until they meet one.\"\n\nThe solution may lie in compromise. The government now pays for fences and trained guard dogs, and farmers receive compensation for losses. Even in the **slack** winter months, the debate continues. The balance is **fragile**, but the village's **geographical** isolation has taught its people to adapt."},"17":{"w":["suppress","systematically","terrify","warning","aggression","agreeable","bizarre","clip","comply","disagreement","disruptive","divert","endow","essence","ethnic","facet","follower","ideology","impulse","melody"],"title":"How Short Videos Changed Music","t":"A few years ago, an old folk **melody** from a small **ethnic** community in southwest China suddenly became famous around the world. A young singer had posted a fifteen-second **clip** of it online, and within a week it had been viewed more than a hundred million times. The story shows how short-video platforms have transformed the music industry.\n\nThe **essence** of these platforms is speed. Users scroll quickly, acting on **impulse**, and only the most catchy songs survive. Some musicians now **systematically** design their songs for short videos, placing the most memorable moment in the first few seconds. Others find the trend **disruptive**, complaining that it rewards novelty rather than skill. A song with a **bizarre** dance or funny lyrics can attract more **followers|follower** than a beautifully written album.\n\nThe trend has also raised serious concerns. Critics argue that the platforms **divert** young people's attention from schoolwork and **suppress** deeper forms of creativity. Some governments have issued a **warning** about the influence of algorithms, which may promote extreme content or political **ideology**. In some cases, angry online comments have turned into **aggression**, with users attacking artists they dislike. These scenes can **terrify** young performers.\n\nNot everyone agrees on what should be done. There is strong **disagreement** about whether platforms should be regulated or left to the market. Companies say they **comply** with all local laws and remove harmful content quickly.\n\nStill, the change has its **agreeable** side. Traditional music from remote regions now reaches global audiences, and artists who were once unknown can build careers. Every **facet** of the industry, from songwriting to marketing, has been touched. As one musician said, \"Technology does not **endow** a song with meaning. But it can help the world hear it.\""},"18":{"w":["naive","negotiate","overlap","overwhelming","persistent","pledge","polar","precedent","premature","prevalence","propagate","questionable","recipe","renovation","representation","simulate","stagger","stall","substantially","tightly"],"title":"Racing Against the Melting Ice","t":"Scientists have long warned that the **polar** regions are warming faster than any other part of the planet. New data show that the pace of change is **overwhelming**: the Arctic has lost a **substantially** larger share of its summer ice than models predicted, and the numbers **stagger** even experienced researchers.\n\nTo understand what is happening, scientists use powerful computers to **simulate** the movement of ice and ocean currents. Their models **overlap** in many respects, and nearly all show the same trend. Yet some politicians still call the evidence **questionable**. Experts argue that it would be **naive** to ignore it. The **prevalence** of extreme weather around the world, they say, is a clear sign of a changing climate.\n\nInternational efforts to respond have often **stalled|stall**. Countries **negotiate** for years over targets, and many promises are broken. When nations **pledge** to cut emissions, critics say, they rarely tie their plans **tightly** to concrete action. A **persistent** problem is that richer countries, which produced most of the emissions, are reluctant to pay for change in poorer ones.\n\nSome progress has been made. One small country set an important **precedent** by passing a law requiring the **renovation** of all public buildings to cut energy use. Other governments are now following its example. Scientists hope such policies will **propagate** quickly across borders.\n\nThere is no simple **recipe** for success, but experts agree that delay is dangerous. Declaring victory now would be **premature**. As one climate scientist said, the melting ice is not just a scientific **representation** of risk on a chart. \"It is a message,\" she said, \"and we cannot afford to ignore it.\""},"19":{"w":["trivial","violation","yielding","applaud","ballet","bug","contradict","criterion","cruise","curb","delusion","dissatisfaction","expedition","extracurricular","fantastic","forum","grab","humidity","inflation","initially"],"title":"Are After-School Activities Worth It?","t":"In many cities, children's lives are now filled with **extracurricular** classes: **ballet** on Monday, piano on Wednesday, coding on Friday. Parents hope these activities will build skills and help their children stand out. **Initially**, the trend seemed harmless. But a growing number of experts now question whether children are doing too much.\n\nAt a recent parents' **forum**, opinions were divided. Some parents **applaud** the classes, saying they keep children busy and teach discipline. Others expressed deep **dissatisfaction**, complaining that the cost keeps rising with **inflation** while the benefits are hard to measure. \"My son does six activities,\" one mother said. \"He has no time just to be a child.\"\n\nResearch seems to **contradict** the common belief that more is always better. Psychologists say that children need free time to rest and play, and that constant pressure can damage their health. Some parents, they warn, live under the **delusion** that every hobby must lead to a prize. Being **yielding** to every new trend is not good parenting; families need a clear **criterion** for choosing activities.\n\nExperiences outside the classroom can still be valuable. One school organises a yearly **expedition** to a rainforest, where students study insects in heat and **humidity** that most have never experienced. Students who were afraid of every **bug** come home wanting to become biologists. Another school takes its students on a river **cruise** to learn about local history. Such trips, teachers say, are **fantastic** for building confidence.\n\nExperts suggest that schools should **curb** excessive homework and that parents should not **grab** every opportunity simply because others do. Ignoring a child's need for rest is not a **trivial** matter; some even call it a **violation** of children's rights. Sometimes, the best activity is no activity at all."},"20":{"w":["molecular","negligible","opaque","premium","ranch","recession","regime","revision","spice","therapy","alteration","fracture","hip","obese","shipwreck","adore","alternate","biased","cherish","compliment"],"title":"Back on His Feet","t":"At seventy-eight, Frank Miller still runs the cattle **ranch** his grandfather built. Last winter, however, he slipped on ice and suffered a **fracture** of his left **hip**. For a man who has worked outdoors his whole life, being stuck in bed was the hardest challenge he had faced.\n\nHip injuries are common among older people, and the risk is higher for those who are **obese** or inactive. Doctors explained that recent advances in **molecular** biology have improved our understanding of how bones heal, and that the risk from surgery was **negligible**. After the operation, Frank began physical **therapy**. His daily **regime** involved walking with support and exercises on **alternate** days to rebuild strength.\n\nThe financial side was harder. Frank's insurance **premium** had risen sharply during the economic **recession**, when beef prices fell and the ranch struggled. The insurance rules were so **opaque** that his daughter spent days reading them. A recent **revision** of the policy, it turned out, did not cover some treatments. Frank complained that the system was **biased** against rural patients, and he wrote to his local newspaper.\n\nRecovery also required **alterations|alteration** to his home. His son added handrails and a ramp, and his granddaughter, who **adores|adore** him, visited every weekend to cook his favourite dishes, full of **spice**. In the evenings, Frank told her stories about his youth, including a **shipwreck** he survived while working on a fishing boat at nineteen.\n\nSix months later, Frank was back on his horse. He paid the nurses a warm **compliment**, saying they had given him his life back. \"I **cherish** every morning now,\" he said. \"When you lose something, even for a while, you learn its value.\""},"21":{"w":["contemplate","correctly","drawback","elevation","erupt","evoke","exemplify","extract","formulate","hamper","idiot","inaccurate","incapable","incredible","incur","instability","integral","inventory","mammal","manifest"],"title":"Living Beside a Volcano","t":"Mount Etna in Sicily is one of the most active volcanoes on Earth. It can **erupt** several times a year, sending lava down its slopes. Yet more than a million people live near it. Visitors often **contemplate** the same question: why would anyone choose to live in such a dangerous place?\n\nThe answer is partly economic. Volcanic soil is extremely rich, and farmers grow excellent grapes, lemons and olives on Etna's lower slopes. Lava fields at higher **elevation** attract tourists, and the volcano has become **integral** to the local identity. Its dark slopes **evoke** both fear and pride.\n\nScientists monitor the volcano constantly. They use satellites, sensors and gas samples to detect signs of **instability** deep underground. Rising levels of certain gases can **manifest** themselves days before an eruption. Predicting eruptions **correctly**, however, remains difficult, and early forecasts are sometimes **inaccurate**. Researchers continue to **formulate** better models. Bad weather and the cost of equipment can **hamper** their work.\n\nThe volcano also affects wildlife. Biologists recently completed an **inventory** of animals living on Etna and found an **incredible** variety of insects, birds and small **mammal** species. Some plants seem to grow only in fresh lava fields. Scientists **extract** soil samples to study how life returns after an eruption. Etna, they say, is a living laboratory.\n\nLiving near a volcano has an obvious **drawback**: occasional destruction. Farmers sometimes **incur** heavy losses, and some villages have been damaged several times. \"Only an **idiot** ignores the warnings,\" one local guide joked. But residents have learned to adapt. Etna seems to **exemplify** a simple truth: people are rarely **incapable** of living with risk when the rewards are great enough."},"22":{"w":["municipal","nonetheless","notable","notwithstanding","pest","predecessor","probe","proximity","rejection","retirement","shrewd","spectacular","subjective","symmetrical","thereafter","thermal","transaction","transcend","unfold","uphold"],"title":"A Library for the Whole City","t":"When the city council announced plans to replace its old central library, many residents were worried. The building's **predecessor**, built in 1920, was much loved, and the first new design received a firm **rejection** from the public. **Nonetheless**, the council pressed on, and five years later, the new library has become one of the most **notable** buildings in the country.\n\nThe design is **spectacular**. Its glass front is perfectly **symmetrical**, and its roof rises gently like an open book. Of course, beauty is **subjective**, and some critics still call it cold. **Notwithstanding** such complaints, visitor numbers have tripled. The building also uses **thermal** energy from underground to heat its reading rooms, cutting energy bills by half.\n\nBuilding it was not easy. Its **proximity** to the river raised fears of flooding, and engineers had to design special foundations. A **municipal** inquiry was launched to **probe** rising costs after a questionable **transaction** with one construction company. The **shrewd** mayor survived the scandal by publishing every contract online, a step that helped **uphold** public trust.\n\nInside, the library offers far more than books. There are music rooms, a children's theatre and a laboratory for repairing old manuscripts, many of which had been damaged by insects. Staff work hard to protect the collection from every kind of **pest**, from beetles to mice.\n\nThe opening ceremony also marked the **retirement** of the chief librarian, who had worked there for forty years. She watched the first visitors arrive and smiled as the day began to **unfold**. \"A good library must **transcend** its walls,\" she said. **Thereafter**, she promised, she would visit every week, this time as a reader."},"23":{"w":["versatile","violate","admiration","aerospace","analyst","assertive","asset","augment","bid","clinic","commonplace","conceive","consultancy","counsel","cozy","default","delicacy","detach","dioxide","discern"],"title":"From Rockets to Clean Air","t":"For fifteen years, Dr. Anna Lin worked in the **aerospace** industry, designing engines for satellites. She was a **versatile** engineer and a valuable **asset** to her company. But when her young daughter developed asthma, Anna began to **conceive** a different career.\n\nVisiting her mother's small medical **clinic** in an industrial town, she saw how common breathing problems had become. Children with coughs were **commonplace**, and doctors could only **counsel** families to stay indoors on bad days. Anna was shocked. Many local factories seemed to **violate** pollution laws, releasing sulphur **dioxide** and other gases into the air.\n\nAnna left her job and founded a small company that builds filters to **detach** harmful particles from factory smoke. It was a risky **bid**. Banks feared the company might **default** on its loans, and an investment **analyst** told her the market was too small. But Anna was **assertive**. She won a contract with a **consultancy** that advised factories on environmental rules, and slowly, orders began to grow.\n\nHer filters do more than clean the air. They also **augment** factory efficiency by capturing useful materials that can be sold. Experts say it can be hard to **discern** an immediate effect, but local air quality has improved noticeably. Anna's work has earned wide **admiration**, and she has been invited to speak at international conferences.\n\nToday, her office is a **cozy** room above a noodle shop, where the owner sells a local **delicacy** of spicy fish soup. Anna often eats there with her team. \"Rockets taught me how to solve hard problems,\" she says. \"Now I solve problems people can feel.\""},"24":{"w":["displace","dwarf","edible","energetic","exceptional","gamble","genetics","haul","hierarchy","hindrance","howl","ignorance","imaginative","inference","inflate","inject","justification","lobby","logistics","maritime"],"title":"Should We Eat Insects?","t":"In many parts of the world, insects have long been part of the diet. Crickets, beetles and silkworms are **edible**, rich in protein and cheap to produce. Some experts believe that insect farming could feed a growing population while using far less land and water than cattle. Yet in Western countries, the idea still makes many people **howl** with disgust.\n\nSupporters argue that our dislike of insects is based on **ignorance**, not taste. They point out that insects sit at the bottom of a food **hierarchy** shaped by culture rather than nutrition. **Imaginative** chefs are now using insect flour to make pasta and biscuits, and some products have proved surprisingly popular among **energetic** young consumers.\n\nThe industry is still small. Insect farms are **dwarfed|dwarf** by traditional meat producers, and building them is a **gamble**. Investors who **inject** money into the sector must deal with complex **logistics**, since insects must be kept warm and transported carefully. Most feed is imported, so companies depend on **maritime** shipping to **haul** raw materials across oceans. Scientists are also studying insect **genetics** to breed faster-growing species.\n\nCritics raise several concerns. Some fear that companies may **inflate** the environmental benefits to attract investors. Others warn that powerful farming groups will **lobby** governments to block new rules. Even supporters admit that public acceptance remains the biggest **hindrance**.\n\nStill, the **justification** for change is strong. Some studies suggest that insect protein could eventually **displace** a large share of the soy used to feed farm animals. The most reasonable **inference**, one researcher says, is that insects will become a normal part of animal feed long before they appear on most dinner plates. Their potential, she adds, is **exceptional**."},"25":{"w":["minimal","minimize","moderately","multitude","myth","notorious","odor","offset","offspring","overestimate","prospective","quest","regiment","resemblance","revive","scrutiny","sculpture","sensitivity","simultaneously","solitary"],"title":"The Quiet World of Solitary Bees","t":"When people think of bees, they usually imagine a busy hive organised like a military **regiment**, with thousands of workers serving a single queen. But this picture is a **myth**. Of the twenty thousand bee species in the world, most are **solitary**: each female builds her own small nest and raises her **offspring** alone.\n\nSolitary bees are often overlooked because they bear little **resemblance** to the honeybee. Some are tiny and black; others look like flies. Yet they are extremely important. A **multitude** of crops, from apples to tomatoes, depend on them for pollination. Some species are attracted to the **odor** of particular flowers, and they visit those flowers with remarkable precision.\n\nUnfortunately, their numbers are falling. Scientists point to the loss of wild flowers and the use of pesticides, especially a group of chemicals that has become **notorious** for harming insects. Bees have a high **sensitivity** to these chemicals; even a **moderately** small dose can affect their ability to find their way home. These pesticides are now under close **scrutiny** in many countries.\n\nThe good news is that solitary bees are easy to help. Gardeners can **minimize** the use of chemicals and leave patches of bare soil for nesting. Even a **minimal** effort, such as planting a few wild flowers, can make a difference. Experts warn, however, that we should not **overestimate** what gardens alone can achieve; farm policy must change **simultaneously**.\n\nSome towns have joined the **quest** to **revive** bee populations. One city commissioned a giant bee **sculpture** to raise awareness, and **prospective** homeowners there can apply for free \"bee hotels\". Small actions like these, scientists say, can help to **offset** decades of decline."},"26":{"w":["stubborn","sturdy","tactics","thesis","trademark","turmoil","unanimous","vicious","wield","witty","anniversary","appraisal","arch","converse","courtesy","degrade","diesel","dilemma","diplomatic","dwell"],"title":"The Old Bridge","t":"In the centre of our town stands a stone bridge built three hundred years ago. Its five graceful arches cross the river, and the central **arch** is so wide that boats can pass beneath it easily. Last spring, the town celebrated the bridge's three-hundredth **anniversary** with music, fireworks and a parade.\n\nThe celebration hid a **dilemma**. For decades, heavy lorries burning **diesel** have crossed the bridge every day, and engineers warn that the vibration could slowly **degrade** the stone. A recent **appraisal** by experts concluded that, although the bridge is remarkably **sturdy**, it cannot carry modern traffic forever.\n\nThe debate that followed caused real **turmoil** in the town council. Shop owners were **stubborn**: they argued that closing the bridge to lorries would destroy local business. Conservationists replied that the bridge is the town's **trademark**, its image printed on every postcard. One student even wrote a university **thesis** arguing that losing the bridge would cost more in tourism than any traffic plan.\n\nBoth sides used clever **tactics**. Shop owners organised a petition, while conservationists invited a famous historian to give a talk. The historian was **witty** and charming, and her talk changed many minds. Some online comments were **vicious**, but most people treated each other with **courtesy**. The mayor, known for being **diplomatic**, invited both groups to **converse** over dinner.\n\nIn the end, the council reached a **unanimous** decision: lorries will use a new road around the town, while the bridge will be open only to pedestrians and cyclists. Those who **wield** power do not always listen, residents said, but this time they did. \"We should not **dwell** on our disagreements,\" the mayor said. \"The bridge has survived three centuries. Now it is our job to protect it.\""},"27":{"w":["greed","hospitality","hurricane","intersection","likeness","migrant","monopoly","morality","outrage","shrub","snobbish","solo","symphony","turtle","alignment","allege","ambassador","analytical","aspiration","bacon"],"title":"After the Hurricane","t":"When a powerful **hurricane** struck a small island in the Caribbean last year, it destroyed homes, roads and power lines. For weeks, much of the island had no electricity. Yet the disaster also revealed the remarkable **hospitality** of its people, who opened their homes to neighbours and strangers alike.\n\nThe storm caused particular damage to the island's beaches, where endangered sea **turtles|turtle** lay their eggs. Volunteers worked day and night to protect the nests, replanting every **shrub** that had been torn from the sand dunes. Among them were many **migrant** workers from nearby islands, who had come to the island in search of work and stayed to help.\n\nRecovery soon raised questions of **morality**. The island's only power company held a **monopoly** on electricity, and when it raised prices after the storm, there was public **outrage**. Critics went on to **allege** that the company's **greed** had delayed repairs in poorer areas. The government promised an investigation, and an **analytical** report later confirmed that wealthy districts had been reconnected first.\n\nHelp also came from abroad. The country's **ambassador** to the United Nations appealed for aid, and an orchestra in New York performed a **symphony** to raise money. The concert ended with a moving **solo** by a young violinist who had grown up on the island. Critics who had dismissed the event as **snobbish** were silenced by its success.\n\nA year later, the island is rebuilding. In a small café near the busiest **intersection** in town, the owner still serves free coffee and **bacon** sandwiches to volunteers. On the wall hangs a painted **likeness** of her late father, who founded the café. Her **aspiration** is simple: a stronger island, in **alignment** with nature rather than against it."},"28":{"w":["bewilder","bleak","bluntly","bureaucracy","bypass","casualty","charm","charter","cling","closet","coincidence","compensate","concentrated","consecutive","consolidate","constitute","consultant","contend","convene","corps"],"title":"When the River Rose","t":"Last July, after five **consecutive** days of heavy rain, the river burst its banks and flooded the lower part of our city. Thousands of people were forced to leave their homes. Fortunately, the number of **casualty** reports remained low, thanks largely to the quick work of rescuers.\n\nThe scene was **bleak**. Streets turned into rivers, and residents had to **cling** to rooftops while waiting for boats. A volunteer **corps** of students and retired workers joined the professional teams, carrying food and medicine to people trapped in their homes. Many said that the speed and **concentrated** effort of the response would **bewilder** anyone who had expected chaos.\n\nAfterwards, however, anger grew. Residents complained that **bureaucracy** had slowed down emergency payments. \"We filled in forms for weeks,\" one shop owner said **bluntly**. \"Nobody wanted to **compensate** us.\" The city council decided to **convene** an emergency meeting and hired an outside **consultant** to review its response.\n\nThe report found that the flood was not simply a **coincidence** of bad weather. Poor planning and new buildings on the riverbank **constitute** a serious risk, it said. Some council members **contend** that the city must stop building on low land altogether. Others want to **consolidate** existing defences and build a new **bypass** channel to carry floodwater around the city centre.\n\nThe city has since drawn up a new flood **charter**, setting out the rights of residents and the duties of the authorities. Meanwhile, life has returned to normal. The old town has regained its **charm**, and families are slowly rebuilding. Many have bought waterproof boxes for their valuables, kept ready in a hall **closet**, just in case."},"29":{"w":["couch","cradle","currency","dazzle","decisive","degenerate","desperately","dessert","destine","deviate","disproportionate","disregard","distinctly","diversion","doctrine","doom","duplicate","edit","eject","elicit"],"title":"Making a Documentary","t":"Last year, I spent six months helping a team make a documentary about Mesopotamia, often called the **cradle** of civilisation. Our director was a quiet woman who believed that history should **dazzle** viewers, not bore them. Her goal was to **elicit** wonder, she said, not to lecture.\n\nThe work was harder than I expected. We filmed in deserts, museums and ancient cities, often working **desperately** to finish before sunset. Every shot had to be planned carefully, because we could not **duplicate** scenes once we had left a site. Back in the studio, we spent weeks trying to **edit** hundreds of hours of film into ninety minutes.\n\nThe director had strict rules. She refused to **deviate** from the evidence or to **disregard** the views of local historians. She disliked films that gave a **disproportionate** amount of time to kings and wars. Instead, she wanted to show how ordinary people lived: how they traded using early forms of **currency**, what they ate, and even what sweet **dessert** they served at festivals. She also explored religious **doctrine** and why so many ancient cities seemed **destined|destine** to fall.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. Once, a security guard tried to **eject** us from a site because our permit was in the wrong name. Another time, our camera broke, and we feared the whole project was **doomed|doom**. But the director was **decisive**. She found a solution within hours.\n\nWhen the film was finally broadcast, I watched it at home on the **couch** with my family. I could **distinctly** remember every difficult day of filming. Some critics feared that history documentaries had begun to **degenerate** into cheap entertainment, but ours proved that serious history can still be a welcome **diversion** from everyday life."},"30":{"w":["embody","ensue","envisage","equate","excessively","experimentation","expire","exquisite","fake","fascinating","fiber","gathering","generosity","graze","immigration","inadequate","indignation","inherent","innumerable","instructor"],"title":"The Art of Silk","t":"For more than two thousand years, silk has been one of China's most **exquisite** products. Its smooth **fiber** comes from the cocoons of silkworms, which feed only on mulberry leaves. Each cocoon contains a single thread up to a kilometre long, and **innumerable** cocoons are needed to make one dress.\n\nIn a small town in southern China, an old weaving workshop still produces silk by hand. Its chief **instructor**, Mrs. Zhou, has taught the craft for forty years. She believes that traditional silk can **embody** a whole culture. \"Every pattern tells a story,\" she says, as she shows visitors a **fascinating** design of cranes and clouds.\n\nThe industry faces serious challenges. Cheap **fake** silk made from plastic fibres floods the market, and many buyers **equate** low price with good value. Mrs. Zhou feels deep **indignation** at the copies. \"There is an **inherent** difference between real silk and plastic,\" she says. \"You can feel it with your hand.\" Government support for traditional craft, she believes, remains **inadequate**.\n\nYoung designers are trying to help. Some have begun **experimentation** with new colours and modern clothing styles, and they **envisage** a future in which traditional silk becomes fashionable again. Others worry that changing the craft **excessively** could destroy what makes it special. Debate often **ensues|ensue** at the workshop's annual **gathering** of weavers, who come from across the region.\n\nThe workshop's history is also a story of **immigration**. In the 1930s, a group of weavers fled war in the north and settled here, bringing their skills with them. Local families welcomed them with great **generosity**. Today, sheep **graze** on the hills above the town, and the old mulberry fields remain. Mrs. Zhou's licence to run the workshop will **expire** next year, but she plans to renew it. \"As long as my hands work,\" she says, \"I will weave.\""},"31":{"w":["intellect","intensify","intricacy","invaluable","ion","irony","irrespective","jealousy","junction","junk","lodging","lofty","longing","malicious","manipulate","marvel","membership","modification","momentum","mortgage"],"title":"Treasure in the Junk Pile","t":"Every year, millions of old phones and laptops end up as **junk**. Inside each one is a small lithium-**ion** battery containing **invaluable** metals such as cobalt and nickel. A young engineer named Chen Hao believes these batteries are a hidden treasure, and his company is gaining **momentum**.\n\nChen started the business five years ago in a small town at a busy railway **junction**, where goods from across the country pass through. To raise money, he took out a second **mortgage** on his house. His friends thought he was crazy. Recycling batteries is difficult because of the **intricacy** of their design: each battery must be opened carefully, and a single mistake can cause a fire.\n\nChen's **intellect** and patience proved decisive. After many tests, he made a small **modification** to an existing machine that allowed it to separate metals safely and cheaply. Visitors to his factory **marvel** at how quickly old batteries are turned into raw materials. The company now offers free **lodging** to workers from distant villages and runs a recycling **membership** scheme for local shops.\n\nSuccess has also brought problems. As competition began to **intensify**, rivals spread **malicious** rumours that his products were unsafe. Chen believes **jealousy** was behind the attacks. Some larger companies have also tried to **manipulate** metal prices to drive small firms out of business. The **irony**, Chen says, is that the industry claims to care about the environment while fighting those who clean it up.\n\nChen's goals are **lofty**. He wants every battery in China to be recycled, **irrespective** of its size or origin. When asked what drives him, he speaks of a deep **longing** to leave the world cleaner than he found it. \"Our grandparents dug these metals out of the ground,\" he says. \"We should not throw them away.\""},"32":{"w":["narrative","paradoxically","peril","perish","perpetual","ponder","proceeding","prolong","propel","proposition","qualification","rally","rating","reckless","reclaim","recurrence","repay","replacement","resent","residential"],"title":"Saving the Wetland","t":"On the edge of a fast-growing city lies a wide wetland, home to thousands of migrating birds. Five years ago, a developer proposed to **reclaim** the land and build a large **residential** area with ten thousand homes. The **proposition** divided the city.\n\nSupporters said the city needed housing and that the project would **propel** economic growth. They told a simple **narrative** of progress: new homes, new jobs and new roads. **Paradoxically**, however, the plan threatened to make the city less safe. Scientists warned that wetlands absorb floodwater, and that destroying them would increase the **peril** of floods and lead to a **recurrence** of the disasters the city had suffered in the past.\n\nResidents organised a large **rally** outside city hall, and environmental groups began legal **proceedings|proceeding** against the developer. Experts with strong **qualifications|qualification** in ecology gave evidence in court. They argued that the plan was **reckless** and that thousands of birds would **perish** if their habitat disappeared. The developer offered to create a **replacement** wetland elsewhere, but scientists said a new one would take decades to mature.\n\nThe case dragged on for three years. Lawyers on both sides tried to **prolong** the process, and many residents began to **resent** the endless delays. Meanwhile, the developer's credit **rating** fell, and it struggled to **repay** its loans.\n\nIn the end, the court ruled that the wetland must be protected. The city now plans to build housing on unused industrial land instead. Many residents still **ponder** how close they came to losing the wetland. Protecting nature, one activist said, is a **perpetual** struggle: \"We won this time. But we have to keep watching.\""},"33":{"w":["retention","revelation","revolve","robust","saturate","scramble","shatter","sneak","soar","spatial","standardize","stereotype","streamline","suffice","superiority","supervise","tease","terrorist","threshold","tract"],"title":"Behind the Scenes at the Airport","t":"Every day, more than a hundred thousand passengers pass through a major international airport. Most of them see only queues and screens. Behind the scenes, however, a complex security system works to keep everyone safe, and its challenges are growing as passenger numbers continue to **soar**.\n\nSecurity staff must **supervise** a huge flow of people and bags. Modern scanners create detailed **spatial** images of each suitcase, allowing officers to detect dangerous objects in seconds. The goal is to find any **terrorist** threat without slowing down ordinary travellers. If a bag crosses a certain risk **threshold**, it is checked by hand.\n\nAirports have tried to **streamline** the process. Many countries now **standardize** security rules, so passengers know what to expect wherever they fly. A **robust** system also depends on people: staff **retention** is a serious problem, because the work is stressful and poorly paid. One officer said that passengers often **tease** staff about removing their shoes, not realising how tiring the job can be.\n\nExperts also warn about bias. Relying on a **stereotype** about who looks dangerous is both unfair and ineffective, they say; criminals can **sneak** through precisely because they do not fit expectations. Technology offers clear **superiority** here, because machines judge objects, not faces. A recent **revelation** that one airport had been targeting certain nationalities caused public anger.\n\nAs cities grow, new airports are being built on vast **tracts|tract** of land far from the centre. Their designs **revolve** around speed and safety. Still, no system is perfect. On busy holiday mornings, passengers **scramble** for space, the terminal becomes **saturated|saturate** with noise, and a single false alarm can **shatter** the calm. In such moments, technology alone will not **suffice**; calm, well-trained people make the difference."},"34":{"w":["tranquil","transit","unavoidable","universally","validity","vent","vow","ward","wardrobe","warehouse","warfare","alien","ambiguous","arctic","bacterium","banker","bearing","blur","bonus","bump"],"title":"Life in the Frozen North","t":"Two years ago, a team of scientists spent six months at a research station in the **Arctic**. Their aim was to study a **bacterium** that survives in ice at temperatures of minus forty degrees. The project was funded partly by a wealthy **banker** who had a lifelong interest in polar science.\n\nGetting there was an adventure. The team flew north in a small plane, and every **bump** in the air made the researchers nervous. Supplies were stored in a **warehouse** at the edge of the station, and goods in **transit** often took weeks to arrive. Each scientist brought a **wardrobe** of special clothing, but frostbite was almost **unavoidable**.\n\nThe landscape was strangely beautiful. On calm days, the frozen sea was **tranquil** and silent, and the line between ice and sky would **blur** into a single white world. \"It felt **alien**,\" one biologist said, \"like another planet.\" Old radar towers, left over from Cold War **warfare** planning, still stood near the station.\n\nThe research produced exciting but **ambiguous** results. The bacterium seemed able to repair its own cells in extreme cold, a discovery that may have a **bearing** on medicine. Other scientists questioned the **validity** of the findings, however, and further tests are needed. Not every idea is **universally** accepted at first.\n\nLife at the station was hard. When one member fell ill, she spent a week in the station's tiny medical **ward**. People sometimes needed to **vent** their frustration, so the team agreed to hold a weekly meeting where anyone could complain freely. At the end of the mission, each scientist received a small **bonus**. Many made a **vow** to return. \"Once the Arctic gets into your heart,\" one said, \"it never leaves.\""},"35":{"w":["centralize","chord","cleanliness","commend","completion","confer","confidential","conform","contaminate","controversy","crisp","decorative","defy","directory","documentary","dome","empirical","eruption","extinguish","finite"],"title":"Rebuilding the Dome","t":"On a **crisp** autumn evening three years ago, a fire broke out in the roof of a famous cathedral. Within an hour, flames had reached the great **dome**. Firefighters worked through the night to **extinguish** the blaze, and by morning, the main structure had survived. Their bravery was widely **commended|commend**.\n\nThe damage, however, was enormous. Smoke and water had **contaminated|contaminate** paintings and **decorative** stonework inside, and parts of the organ were destroyed. A **documentary** about the fire, broadcast a few months later, showed experts weeping as they examined the ruins.\n\nRebuilding soon caused **controversy**. Some architects wanted a modern glass roof, arguing that new materials would **defy** the risk of future fires. Others insisted that the dome should **conform** exactly to the original design. The debate became heated, almost an **eruption** of national anger, and the government decided to **centralize** all decisions in a single committee.\n\nThe committee relied on **empirical** evidence rather than opinion. It studied old drawings, photographs and a **confidential** engineering report that had never been published. It also compiled a **directory** of skilled craftsmen across the country, since only a **finite** number of people still knew traditional building methods. Experts met regularly to **confer** on each stage of the work.\n\nToday, the project is nearing **completion**. Workers maintain strict **cleanliness** to protect the restored paintings, and the organ has been rebuilt by hand. Last month, during a test, the organist played a single **chord**, and its sound filled the dome once again. Several people in the audience began to cry."},"36":{"w":["flatter","fluctuation","galaxy","guilt","humanitarian","impart","inertia","insistent","insulate","interconnect","leakage","managerial","marsh","masculine","merchandise","merge","monster","novelty","orchard","preach"],"title":"A Festival of Stars","t":"Most people living in cities have never seen the **galaxy** we live in. Light from streets and buildings hides all but the brightest stars. That is why a small village in the hills has started an annual \"dark-sky festival\", which has grown from a local **novelty** into a national event.\n\nThe festival takes place in an apple **orchard** at the edge of the village, near a quiet **marsh** where frogs sing all night. Visitors bring blankets to **insulate** themselves from the cold ground and lie back to watch the Milky Way. Children dress up as aliens and friendly **monsters|monster**, and a stall sells festival **merchandise** such as star maps and T-shirts.\n\nThe organiser, a retired teacher, combines a love of science with strong **managerial** skills. When two neighbouring villages decided to **merge** their events, she found ways to **interconnect** the programmes, with buses running between sites. Visitor numbers show some **fluctuation**, depending on the weather, but the trend is upwards. Profits go to a **humanitarian** charity that supports schools in poor regions.\n\nThe festival has a serious message. Light pollution wastes energy, disturbs wildlife and harms human sleep. One major cause is the **leakage** of light from badly designed street lamps. Yet change is slow, partly because of official **inertia**. Organisers try not to **preach**, and they do not want visitors to feel **guilt** about their own lights. Instead, they aim to **impart** wonder.\n\nThey also want to challenge the idea that astronomy is a **masculine** hobby. Many of the volunteer guides are women, and several girls have since started astronomy clubs at school. Professional photographs may **flatter** the night sky, the organiser admits, but nothing compares to seeing it with your own eyes. Her message is gentle but **insistent**: \"Turn off the lights, and look up.\""},"37":{"w":["proficient","rectangular","retrospect","ridicule","scrap","senator","spokesman","suspension","temperament","texture","thanksgiving","tickle","tug","unlimited","unorthodox","vaccinate","vigor","abolish","abrupt","acquaint"],"title":"The Doctor Who Broke the Rules","t":"In a remote mountain region, many parents once refused to **vaccinate** their children. Rumours spread that vaccines were dangerous, and clinics stood empty. Then Dr. Maria Santos arrived, and within five years, vaccination rates had risen from forty to ninety per cent.\n\nHer methods were **unorthodox**. Instead of lecturing parents, she visited villages with a guitar and a bag of toys. She would **tickle** nervous children until they laughed and let them play with her instruments before giving them an injection. She spoke the local language fluently and quickly became **proficient** in the customs of each village, taking time to **acquaint** herself with every family.\n\nNot everyone approved. Some colleagues would **ridicule** her approach, calling it unprofessional. After a complaint, she received a three-month **suspension** from her post, which came as an **abrupt** shock to the villages. Parents organised protests, and a local **senator** took up her case. A **spokesman** for the health ministry later admitted that the suspension had been a mistake.\n\nIn **retrospect**, her success seems obvious. Dr. Santos had an ideal **temperament** for the job: patient, warm and full of **vigor**. She worked from a small **rectangular** clinic made from **scrap** wood and metal, and she seemed to have **unlimited** energy. She also campaigned to **abolish** the fees that had kept poor families away.\n\nEvery year, the villages hold a **thanksgiving** dinner in her honour. Families bring bread with a rough, homemade **texture** and dishes from their own gardens. As she leaves, small children **tug** at her coat and beg her to come back soon. \"This is the best medicine,\" she says, smiling."},"38":{"w":["addict","adoption","affirm","agitation","allegation","alliance","ally","amid","amusement","analogous","analytic","annually","appraise","arc","astonishment","astronomy","attachment","attorney","avail","avert"],"title":"Are Video Games Addictive?","t":"Video games are one of the most popular forms of **amusement** in the world, and the industry now earns more **annually** than films and music combined. Yet concerns about game addiction are growing. Some parents describe their child as an **addict**, unable to stop playing even when they want to.\n\nResearchers are divided. Some **affirm** that excessive gaming is a real disorder, **analogous** to gambling addiction. Others argue that most players have a healthy **attachment** to games and that labelling them as sick does more harm than good. An **analytic** review of fifty studies found that only a small minority of players show serious problems, although that minority deserves help.\n\nThe debate has moved into the courts. Amid growing public **agitation**, a group of parents formed an **alliance** and hired an **attorney** to sue several game companies. Their main **allegation** is that games are deliberately designed to keep players hooked. The companies deny the claims, and some politicians have become their **ally**, warning against unnecessary regulation.\n\nGovernments are now trying to **appraise** the risks. Several countries have announced the **adoption** of new rules, such as limits on spending in games for young players. Experts hope such measures will **avert** a public health crisis. Many parents, however, feel that warnings are of little **avail** without better education.\n\nGames can also inspire. One popular game about **astronomy** lets players explore real planets, and teachers report the **astonishment** of students who discover the scale of the universe. The story follows a dramatic **arc** from Earth to the edge of the solar system. **Amid** all the worry, it is worth remembering that games can teach as well as distract."},"39":{"w":["badge","bait","ballot","bankrupt","bankruptcy","banquet","barren","beware","bitterness","blunt","breakdown","brutal","buffalo","buzz","cane","cartoon","cereal","chronicle","circulation","coalition"],"title":"The Last Local Newspaper","t":"For more than a century, the Valley Gazette has been the **chronicle** of a small farming town. It has reported on everything from harvests to elections, publishing **ballot** results and analysing each new **coalition** government. But in recent years, its **circulation** has fallen sharply, and last spring it came close to **bankruptcy**.\n\nThe causes are familiar. Advertisers have moved online, and young readers get their news from social media. The paper's editor, a **blunt** old man who walks with a **cane**, provided a **breakdown** of the figures: income had fallen by half in ten years. \"Many papers like ours have gone **bankrupt**,\" he said. \"We are next unless something changes.\"\n\nThe town's economy has suffered too. A **brutal** drought left the land **barren**, and **cereal** farmers lost most of their crops. A nearby **buffalo** ranch was one of the few businesses that survived. In such hard times, residents said, losing the newspaper would feel like losing a friend. Its long-running **cartoon**, which gently mocked local politicians, was especially loved.\n\nYoung journalists have begun to help. They created a website and a podcast, refusing to use misleading headlines as **bait** for clicks. \"Readers should **beware** of free news,\" one reporter said. \"Someone always pays for it.\" Their efforts created a real **buzz**, and hundreds of readers have bought subscriptions.\n\nLast month, the Gazette held a **banquet** to celebrate its anniversary. Each guest received a small **badge** bearing the paper's old logo. There was some **bitterness** about the struggles of recent years, but the mood was hopeful. \"We are still here,\" the editor said, raising his glass."},"40":{"w":["coastal","coincide","commence","commonwealth","compatibility","competence","complement","complication","compulsory","concise","confrontation","constituent","contamination","contemptuous","continental","contrive","cosmos","counseling","counteract","courageous"],"title":"Cleaning Up the Coast","t":"Last summer, an oil tanker ran aground off a **coastal** town in Australia, a member of the **Commonwealth** of Nations. Thousands of tonnes of oil poured into the sea. The disaster happened to **coincide** with the start of the tourist season, and its effects were felt immediately.\n\nThe cleanup was due to **commence** within hours, but there were complications. The equipment sent from different agencies lacked **compatibility**, and each **complication** caused new delays. A **concise** report later found that the authorities had shown poor **competence**, and that rules requiring **compulsory** emergency plans had been ignored.\n\nLocal fishermen were furious. They said the sea was their whole **cosmos**, the centre of their lives and work. When the shipping company's managers gave a **contemptuous** response at a public meeting, a **confrontation** broke out, and police had to calm the crowd. Many residents later received **counseling** to deal with stress.\n\nScientists warned about long-term **contamination**. Certain **constituent** chemicals of oil can remain in the sand for years and enter the food chain. Some oil also drifted towards the **continental** shelf, where many fish breed. To **counteract** the damage, researchers released special bacteria that break down oil naturally. These methods **complement** traditional cleanup work.\n\nHundreds of **courageous** volunteers came from across the country to clean the beaches by hand. Some had to **contrive** clever ways to rescue birds covered in oil, using kitchen tools and baby shampoo. A year later, the beaches are open again, but the town has not forgotten. \"We learned that the sea is fragile,\" one fisherman said, \"and so are we.\""},"41":{"w":["crab","crippling","culminate","cumulative","dealer","deceptive","decidedly","deduct","deficiency","deficient","deform","denial","denote","detector","deviation","diffuse","dilute","disappearance","disapproval","disastrous"],"title":"The Trouble with Crabs","t":"Along the Pacific coast of North America, **crab** fishing has supported families for generations. In recent years, however, fishermen have noticed a worrying change. Many crabs are smaller than before, and some have shells that are thin or strangely shaped.\n\nScientists believe the cause is ocean acidification. As carbon dioxide from the air begins to **diffuse** into seawater, the water becomes more acidic. Even a small **deviation** from normal levels can make it harder for animals to build shells. Young crabs grown in acidic water show a **deficiency** of minerals, and their shells may **deform** as they develop. Each change may seem minor, but the **cumulative** effect over many years could be serious.\n\nThe ocean can appear **deceptively|deceptive** healthy. Water looks clear and blue, yet chemical tests tell a different story. To track the problem, researchers have placed a **detector** on buoys along the coast. Readings in red **denote** dangerous levels. In some bays, the water is **decidedly** more acidic than it was twenty years ago.\n\nFor fishermen, the consequences could be **crippling**. One seafood **dealer** reported that prices have become unstable, and many families now **deduct** losses from their savings each season. Some scientists fear the trend will **culminate** in the **disappearance** of entire crab populations, a **disastrous** outcome for coastal towns.\n\nDespite the evidence, there is still some **denial**. A few politicians claim the science is **deficient** and that changes are natural. Their views have met strong **disapproval** from researchers. Experts point out that the ocean cannot simply **dilute** unlimited amounts of carbon. Cutting emissions, they say, remains the only real solution."},"42":{"w":["dispense","displacement","distil","distortion","dizzy","downturn","dreadful","dubious","ego","elapse","eloquent","embark","embroidery","enlighten","entity","equator","escort","eternal","evaporation","exclamation"],"title":"Crossing the Equator","t":"Last year, I joined a sailing ship on a voyage from Lisbon to Brazil. Fifty passengers were due to **embark** in Portugal, and none of us knew exactly what to expect. The company that owned the ship, a small private **entity**, had struggled during the tourism **downturn**, and the ship itself was over eighty years old.\n\nThe captain was an **eloquent** woman who loved to **enlighten** passengers about sailing history. She explained how old ships measured their size by **displacement**, the weight of water they push aside. Her stories were full of humour and free of **ego**; she admitted that she still felt **dizzy** during rough seas.\n\nLife on board was simple. Fresh water was limited, so the crew would **dispense** it carefully each morning. They also used a small machine to **distil** seawater, removing the salt through **evaporation**. One passenger, a retired seamstress, spent her days working on an elaborate piece of **embroidery** showing waves and dolphins.\n\nThe weather was often **dubious**. One night, a **dreadful** storm hit the ship, and a small navy vessel was sent to **escort** us to calmer water. In the heat that followed, the air above the sea created a strange **distortion**, making distant clouds look like mountains.\n\nThree weeks had to **elapse** before we finally reached the **equator**. When the captain announced the crossing, the passengers let out a loud **exclamation** of joy, and an old tradition followed: everyone who had never crossed before was splashed with seawater. Standing on deck that evening, I felt that the sea was **eternal**, much older and larger than any of our worries."},"43":{"w":["exempt","exposition","extraction","extravagant","feminine","ferry","fitting","fixture","fluctuate","foam","fraud","fright","frightful","fro","fuss","glorify","grim","hail","ham","handicap"],"title":"A Day at the World Expo","t":"This summer, my sister and I visited the world **exposition** in a large coastal city. More than a hundred countries had built pavilions, each designed to show off its culture and technology. To get there, we took a **ferry** across the bay, watching white **foam** rise behind the boat.\n\nThe pavilions were often **extravagant**. One was shaped like a giant flower, with soft, **feminine** curves; another looked like a spaceship. A pavilion about energy explained the **extraction** of oil and gas, though some visitors felt it tried to **glorify** an industry that harms the environment. A more **fitting** exhibit nearby showed how wind and solar power could replace fossil fuels.\n\nNot everything went smoothly. In the afternoon, a sudden storm brought **hail** the size of marbles, giving many visitors a real **fright**. People ran to and **fro** looking for shelter, and the noise of the ice on the glass roof was **frightful**. Staff, however, did not make a **fuss**; they handed out towels and **ham** sandwiches until the storm passed.\n\nWe also learned about the expo's darker side. A **grim** news report revealed a ticket **fraud**: thousands of visitors had bought fake tickets online. Meanwhile, ticket prices continued to **fluctuate**, rising sharply at weekends. Some critics complained that the organising company was **exempt** from certain taxes, which they considered unfair.\n\nOverall, the organisers deserved praise. Every pavilion was accessible to people with a physical **handicap**, with ramps and lifts at every entrance. A light display, which had become a nightly **fixture** of the event, ended our day perfectly."},"44":{"w":["harbor","harmonious","hazardous","henceforth","herald","hose","hover","hum","humiliation","ideally","illuminate","immerse","immortal","immunity","impair","implicit","incidentally","inclination","incomplete","industrious"],"title":"Lights Over the Harbour","t":"For decades, the old **harbor** in our city was a place people avoided. Abandoned factories lined the water, and the soil was full of **hazardous** chemicals. The closure of the shipyard in the 1980s was a deep **humiliation** for a city that had once built ships for the world.\n\nLast year, after a long cleanup, the harbour reopened as a park, and the city held a festival of light to **herald** its rebirth. Buildings were **illuminated|illuminate** with colourful images, and drones would **hover** above the water, forming shapes in the sky. Visitors could **immerse** themselves in sound and light shows, while the crowd's low **hum** of excitement filled the night.\n\nThe organisers took safety seriously. Firefighters stood by with a **hose** at every major display, and no fireworks were used near the old fuel tanks. Planners also made sure that smoke from food stalls would not **impair** visibility for boats. \"**Ideally**, people should forget they are standing on a former industrial site,\" one planner said.\n\nThe festival carried an **implicit** message: the city had changed. **Henceforth**, the mayor promised, the harbour would be a place for people, not just for business. Some residents were sceptical, noting that the renovation is still **incomplete** and that several old warehouses remain empty. No city has **immunity** from economic decline, they argued. **Incidentally**, the mayor's own **inclination**, he admitted, had once been to sell the land to developers.\n\nStill, most visitors were delighted. Older residents shared memories of the shipyard's **industrious** workers, whose stories, they said, should be **immortal**. For one evening at least, the harbour felt **harmonious**: old and young, past and present, side by side."},"45":{"w":["infancy","infinitely","ingenious","ingenuity","instantaneous","intact","intellectually","interim","intermittent","intrigue","intrinsic","intuition","invariably","ironically","jean","jerk","jog","lamentation","lengthen","lily"],"title":"The Inventor Next Door","t":"My neighbour Mr. Lee is eighty-two years old, and he has spent his whole life inventing things. He wears the same faded **jean** jacket every day, he goes for a **jog** each morning and he grows a white **lily** by his front door. Children in the street call him \"the professor\".\n\nMr. Lee's garage is full of **ingenious** devices. There is a machine that waters his plants whenever the soil becomes dry, and a lamp that turns on in an **instantaneous** response to clapping. His **ingenuity** is remarkable, considering that he never went to university. \"I trust my **intuition**,\" he says. \"If an idea feels right, I try it.\"\n\nHe began inventing when electronics were still in their **infancy**. Back then, power in his village was **intermittent**, and he built a small generator so his mother could read at night. **Ironically**, his most successful invention, a simple bicycle lock, earned him almost nothing, because a large company copied it. He tells the story without **lamentation**. \"Money was never the point,\" he says.\n\nMr. Lee believes that inventing has **intrinsic** value. It keeps him **intellectually** active, and he is **infinitely** curious about how things work. Visitors are **invariably** surprised by how quickly he understands new technology. His latest project, a device that helps old people stand up without a sudden **jerk**, continues to **intrigue** engineers at a local university.\n\nLast month, a storm damaged his garage roof. While it was being repaired, he moved his workshop into an **interim** space in his kitchen. Remarkably, every machine survived **intact**. Doctors have told him to rest more, but he plans to **lengthen** his working day instead. \"There are still so many things to invent,\" he says."},"46":{"w":["limp","linguistic","literal","litter","loosely","luminous","luxurious","magnify","maiden","mall","manuscript","martyr","masculinity","mastery","maternity","metabolic","microscopic","mighty","mingle","mobilize"],"title":"The Poem Under the Mall","t":"Three years ago, builders digging the foundations of a new shopping **mall** made an unexpected discovery: a stone box containing an ancient **manuscript**. Among broken bricks and modern **litter**, they had found a book of poems more than eight hundred years old.\n\nThe manuscript was in poor condition. Its **limp** leather cover was falling apart, and scientists found that the **metabolic** activity of bacteria had damaged many pages. Some letters were **microscopic**, and researchers had to **magnify** them hundreds of times to read them. Under special light, traces of ink became **luminous**, revealing words that had been invisible for centuries.\n\nThe city quickly moved to **mobilize** experts. A team of translators with deep **linguistic** knowledge began work. They faced a difficult choice: should they produce a **literal** translation, faithful to every word, or translate the poems more **loosely** to capture their beauty? In the end, they did both. Their **mastery** of the old language impressed scholars around the world.\n\nThe poems themselves are remarkable. One tells the story of a young **maiden** who waits for her lover beside a **mighty** river. Another honours a local hero, remembered as a **martyr** who died defending the city. The poems explore ideas of **masculinity** and courage alongside themes of **maternity** and family love. In them, joy and sorrow **mingle** naturally.\n\nToday, the manuscript is displayed in a museum, protected in a glass case. A **luxurious** printed edition has also been published, though the original remains the real treasure. Ironically, the mall was built after all; shoppers now walk above the place where the poems lay hidden for so long."},"47":{"w":["monetary","moss","neighbouring","nickel","obscene","offensive","olive","onward","optimum","overflow","overt","panic","paralysis","peculiarity","periodical","perplex","petition","physiological","plausible","porcelain"],"title":"Water and Olives","t":"In a dry valley in southern Spain, farmers have grown **olive** trees for centuries. Their oil, once stored in large **porcelain** jars, is famous across Europe. But in recent years, the valley has faced a serious water crisis, and its future is uncertain.\n\nThe problem began when a company opened a **nickel** mine in a **neighbouring** valley. Farmers claim that the mine uses huge amounts of water and has polluted the river. During heavy rain, waste from the mine's storage ponds can **overflow** into streams. Old stone walls by the river, once green with **moss**, are now stained brown.\n\nAt first, the local council seemed frozen in **paralysis**, unsure how to respond. Farmers organised a **petition** signed by thousands of people, and the story was reported in a national **periodical**. Some officials made **offensive** remarks, describing the farmers as enemies of progress, which only increased public anger. Protesters accused the company of making **obscene** profits while villages went dry.\n\nScientists were asked to investigate. They found that the trees showed clear **physiological** signs of stress, such as smaller leaves and fewer fruits. One **peculiarity** seemed to **perplex** them: some trees near the mine were healthy, while others far away were dying. The most **plausible** explanation, they concluded, was that underground water channels carried pollution in unexpected directions.\n\nThe case has now reached the courts. The company has offered **monetary** compensation, but farmers say money cannot replace water. \"We do not want **overt** conflict,\" one farmer said. \"We want a fair solution.\" Experts say there is still time to find the **optimum** balance between mining and farming. For now, the farmers continue **onward**, trying not to **panic**, hoping that the rains will come."},"48":{"w":["poultry","precede","predominantly","predominate","pregnancy","prevailing","prevention","principally","printer","prominence","propagation","prototype","provocative","provoke","pumpkin","qualitative","quantitative","rash","rattle","reassure"],"title":"Stopping Bird Flu","t":"Every winter, farmers around the world fear the arrival of bird flu. The virus spreads quickly among **poultry**, and a single case can force a farm to destroy thousands of birds. In some regions, outbreaks **predominantly** affect small family farms, where protection is harder to organise.\n\n**Prevention** depends **principally** on keeping wild birds away from farm animals. Scientists believe that migrating birds play a key role in the **propagation** of the virus, especially when the **prevailing** winds carry them along certain routes. Warm weather usually reduces the number of cases, but outbreaks often **precede** the spring migration.\n\nHealth officials try to **reassure** the public that the risk to humans remains low. However, they advise extra care for vulnerable groups, including people with weak immune systems and women during **pregnancy**. Some newspapers have published **provocative** headlines that **provoke** unnecessary fear. Experts warn that **rash** decisions, such as banning all chicken imports, can damage farmers without improving safety.\n\nResearch is moving forward. Scientists combine **quantitative** data on infection rates with **qualitative** interviews with farmers, hoping to understand which measures work best. One laboratory has developed a **prototype** vaccine for chickens. Another team uses a 3D **printer** to produce cheap testing kits. Such projects have given the issue new **prominence** in public debate.\n\nOn one small farm I visited, the farmer walked among his hens, giving his feed bucket a gentle **rattle**. Rows of **pumpkin** plants filled the field beside the barn. \"We keep everything clean,\" he said. \"Fear should not **predominate**. Good habits are the best medicine.\""},"49":{"w":["reckon","reconcile","recur","redundant","relay","remainder","remnant","renaissance","reproduction","reside","resonance","rig","riot","roam","ruthless","scarlet","scrub","seaside","sheer","sheriff"],"title":"A Seaside Town Comes Back","t":"Thirty years ago, Port Ellis was a busy **seaside** town that depended on oil. Most men worked on an offshore oil **rig**, and the town's shops and bars were full. Then oil prices collapsed, and the company closed its operations. Almost overnight, thousands of workers were made **redundant**.\n\nThe years that followed were hard. Unemployment led to anger, and in 1992 a **riot** broke out after a protest against the company. The local **sheriff**, who had grown up in the town, later said that it was the worst night of his career. Many residents accused the company of being **ruthless**, and some found it difficult to **reconcile** with the past.\n\nToday, however, Port Ellis is enjoying a **renaissance**. Artists were attracted by cheap rents and the **sheer** beauty of the coast, where cliffs rise above the sea and **scarlet** sunsets light up the sky. Many now **reside** in converted warehouses at the heart of a lively art district. A gallery sells high-quality **reproductions|reproduction** of local paintings, and a music festival each summer has strong **resonance** with young people.\n\nNot every problem has gone. Some **remnants|remnant** of the old industry remain, including a rusting pier and empty fuel tanks. Locals **reckon** that about a third of the town still lives in poverty, and they fear that the decline could **recur** if tourism falls. Volunteers **scrub** graffiti from walls and organise beach cleanups.\n\nVisitors can **roam** freely along the dunes, and every September the town holds a **relay** race along the beach. The **remainder** of the year is quieter, but residents are proud. \"We were knocked down,\" one artist said, \"and we stood up again.\""},"50":{"w":["skip","skyscraper","snail","sniff","sofa","spacious","spinal","spiral","stalk","steak","straightforward","strait","stride","subscription","subsequently","sue","suicide","synthesize","tentative","terminate"],"title":"Living in the Sky","t":"In many Asian cities, a growing share of people live and work in a **skyscraper**. Land is scarce, especially in cities built beside a narrow **strait** or on small islands, so buildings grow upwards instead of outwards. For some residents, life in the sky is exciting. For others, it brings new problems.\n\nHousing is the most obvious one. Prices continue to **spiral** upwards, and **spacious** flats are a luxury few can afford. Many young families live in tiny apartments where a **sofa** doubles as a bed. Commuting is also difficult: during rush hour, traffic moves at a **snail**'s pace, and some people **skip** breakfast to arrive on time.\n\nHealth experts have begun to **synthesize** research on the effects of urban life. Office workers who sit for long hours suffer more **spinal** problems, and some have even tried to **sue** their employers over poor working conditions. Doctors also warn about loneliness and stress. Several cities now fund **suicide** prevention hotlines as part of wider mental health programmes, offering support to anyone who feels overwhelmed.\n\nSome solutions are surprisingly **straightforward**. Rooftop gardens allow residents to grow vegetables, and children love to **sniff** the herbs and watch a tomato **stalk** grow taller each week. Companies that **subsequently** introduced walking meetings reported happier staff. Many workers now pay a monthly **subscription** to gyms in their office towers.\n\nProgress is still **tentative**, but cities are making a confident **stride** towards healthier design. One developer recently decided to **terminate** plans for another luxury tower and build a public park instead. Residents celebrated with a picnic, though one joked that he would still prefer a good **steak** dinner to a salad."},"51":{"w":["terrain","thoroughly","tolerant","trader","tread","tremendously","triple","unconventional","unify","unlock","unreasonable","unreliable","valve","vegetation","vein","verdict","vicinity","violently","visa","visualize"],"title":"The Market at the Mountain Pass","t":"High in the mountains between two countries lies a small market town that has connected **traders|trader** for centuries. The **terrain** is harsh: steep slopes, thin air and little **vegetation** beyond hardy grass. Yet every week, hundreds of merchants make the journey to buy and sell wool, tea and jade.\n\nThe journey is not easy. Roads are **unreliable**, and lorries shake **violently** on the rocky tracks. Travellers must **tread** carefully on narrow paths where a single mistake could be fatal. Many bring small gas stoves for cooking, and checking every **valve** before lighting them has become a habit. In the **vicinity** of the town, a famous **vein** of green jade runs through the rocks, attracting buyers from far away.\n\nFor years, trade was limited by complicated rules. Each country had its own customs system, and fees were often **unreasonable**. Traders needed a **visa** that could take months to obtain. Last year, however, the two governments agreed to **unify** their procedures, and a court **verdict** ended a long dispute over tariffs. Since then, trade has grown **tremendously**; some merchants say their sales have **tripled|triple**.\n\nLocal officials want to **unlock** the town's potential further. They have proposed some **unconventional** ideas, such as a mountain festival and a digital market that lets buyers **visualize** products in three dimensions. Not everyone welcomes the changes. Older traders fear that modern methods will destroy traditions they know **thoroughly**.\n\nStill, the market remains a place of exchange in every sense. People of different languages and religions meet here, and they have learned to be **tolerant**. \"In the mountains,\" one old trader said, \"you cannot survive without your neighbours.\""},"52":{"w":["vividly","vocational","void","waitress","warrant","watertight","weaver","wedge","whereby","windy","wither","wrestle","wrinkle","abbreviation","abide","absorption","acquisitive","adjoin","adjustable","advantageous"],"title":"A Second Chance","t":"At thirty-five, Lin Mei had worked for twelve years as a **waitress** in a busy restaurant. Her days were long, her pay was low, and she felt her life had become a kind of **void**. \"I could see a **wrinkle** on my face for every year I'd wasted,\" she says with a laugh. Then she saw an advertisement for a **vocational** training course in traditional weaving.\n\nThe course was run by a programme **whereby** adults could receive free training in skills that are disappearing. Lin had always loved fabric, and she remembers **vividly** her grandmother working at a wooden loom. She applied, and to her surprise, she was accepted. Her absence from work did not **warrant** dismissal, her employer agreed, so she could keep her job part-time.\n\nLearning was hard. Lin had to **wrestle** with complicated patterns and master the use of a wooden **wedge** to keep threads tight. The workshop, which **adjoins|adjoin** an old silk factory, was cold and **windy** in winter. Her teacher, a master **weaver** in her eighties, insisted that students **abide** by strict rules. Every loom had an **adjustable** seat, but the work still made her back ache.\n\nLin's **absorption** in the craft was complete. While other students learned slowly, she progressed quickly, finding it **advantageous** to have years of experience working with her hands. She also learned business skills, such as reading contracts. \"A contract must be **watertight**,\" her teacher warned. \"Never sign anything you don't understand, even an **abbreviation**.\"\n\nToday, Lin runs her own small studio. She is not **acquisitive**; she sells only what she can make by hand. She says her old dreams had begun to **wither**, but weaving brought them back to life."},"53":{"w":["aerial","affective","affiliate","afflict","affluence","alas","album","allegiance","allied","allowance","amateur","ambient","ambiguity","amends","amplitude","analogue","anode","antarctic","antenna","antique"],"title":"Voices Through the Air","t":"Long before smartphones, a small community of **amateur** radio operators was talking to people around the world. Today, the hobby is enjoying a quiet revival. Enthusiasts set up an **antenna** on the roof, or a simple **aerial** in the garden, and spend evenings listening to distant voices.\n\nMy uncle has been a radio amateur for forty years. His study is full of **antique** equipment, including old radios whose glowing tubes contain a small metal **anode**. He prefers **analogue** devices to digital ones, saying they feel more alive. He explains how sound travels in waves, and how a signal's **amplitude** decides how far it can reach. On quiet nights, the **ambient** noise drops, and signals arrive more clearly.\n\nHis proudest achievement came one winter, when he made contact with a research station in the **antarctic**. The scientists there sent him a postcard, which he keeps in an **album** with hundreds of others from around the world. As a boy, he spent his weekly **allowance** on radio parts.\n\nRadio operators have a long history of helping others. During the Second World War, operators in **allied** countries helped pass secret messages, showing great **allegiance** to their cause. Today, many clubs **affiliate** themselves with emergency services, providing communication when disasters **afflict** a region and phone networks fail.\n\nThe hobby is not just technical. My uncle says it has an **affective** side: hearing a stranger's voice across an ocean creates a feeling of connection. In an age of **affluence** and constant online noise, he values its simplicity. Messages can contain **ambiguity**, so operators use clear codes. \"When I make a mistake,\" he says, \"I always make **amends** by apologising on air.\" Then he adds, with a sigh: \"**Alas**, young people prefer their phones.\""},"54":{"w":["antisocial","appendix","appreciable","ascend","ashore","ass","assassinate","assimilate","assimilation","assurance","athletic","attendant","availability","axial","axle","backbone","bald","bandage","bandit","barge"],"title":"An Explorer's Diary","t":"In 1886, a young British explorer named Henry Ward set out to travel up a great river in Central Asia. His diary, recently published with a long **appendix** of maps and letters, offers a vivid picture of the journey and of the man himself.\n\nWard was not an easy companion. He was **antisocial**, often refusing to eat with his team, and he complained constantly. Yet he was also **athletic** and brave. He began the journey on a wooden **barge** pulled by horses, and when the river became too shallow, he went **ashore** and continued on foot, using an **ass** to carry his supplies. When a cart's **axle** broke in the mountains, he repaired it himself.\n\nThe region was dangerous. Only a year earlier, rebels had tried to **assassinate** the local governor, and travellers were often attacked. One night, a group of **bandits|bandit** stole half of Ward's food. In the struggle, his guide was injured, and Ward wrapped his arm in a **bandage** torn from his own shirt. He wrote that the guide, a **bald** old man with remarkable strength, was the **backbone** of the expedition.\n\nAs they **ascended|ascend** into the high mountains, Ward began to change. He learned local languages and tried to **assimilate** into village life. His diary records his thoughts on cultural **assimilation** and his growing respect for the people he met. He also notes the **axial** line of the mountain range, which he mapped with **appreciable** accuracy.\n\nWard never found the lost city he was searching for. But a palace **attendant** who met him later wrote that the explorer had given his **assurance** to return. He did not, partly because of the limited **availability** of funding. His diary, however, has finally completed his journey."},"55":{"w":["barley","barometer","baron","basement","batch","bazaar","bead","beetle","beneficiary","benign","bestow","biotech","bishop","blacksmith","bleach","blink","blond","blouse","blunder","blush"],"title":"Barley and Biotech","t":"For centuries, the village of Haldon has grown **barley** in the fields below an old castle. The land once belonged to a local **baron**, and the village church still contains the tomb of a **bishop** who blessed the harvests in the Middle Ages. Today, however, the village is at the centre of a modern debate about **biotech** farming.\n\nTwo years ago, a young scientist set up a small laboratory in the **basement** of the old **blacksmith**'s workshop. Her goal was to breed barley that resists a **beetle** which has destroyed crops across the region. She tested each **batch** of seeds carefully and monitored the weather with an old **barometer** that once belonged to the blacksmith.\n\nHer results were promising. The new barley seemed **benign**, with no harmful effects on soil or insects, and farmers who used it lost far fewer plants. Some villagers welcomed her work, saying they would be the main **beneficiary**. Others were suspicious. At a public meeting, one farmer accused her of making a terrible **blunder**, and the young scientist began to **blush** as people argued.\n\nThe debate continued at the village's annual **bazaar**, where stalls sell homemade jam, glass **bead** necklaces and old books. An elderly woman in a white **blouse**, her **blond** hair turned grey, defended the scientist. \"My grandmother used to **bleach** linen in the river,\" she said. \"Things change. We must judge new ideas fairly.\"\n\nIn the end, the village voted to continue the trial for another year. The regional government even decided to **bestow** an award on the scientist for her work. She accepted it without a **blink**, then returned to her laboratory. \"The real test,\" she said, \"will be next summer's harvest.\""},"56":{"w":["boiler","bourgeois","boycott","brace","bribe","bridegroom","bridle","brightness","browse","buddhism","bugle","bulletin","burglar","burial","burner","bushel","butt","by-product","calculus","calibration"],"title":"Browsing the Village Bulletin","t":"Last winter, I spent a week in our county archive, trying to **browse** through a hundred years of a village **bulletin**. The weekly newsletter, first printed in 1890, recorded everything from weddings to harvests. Reading it felt like opening a window onto a vanished world.\n\nThe early issues are full of farming news. Prices were measured by the **bushel**, and readers were advised to **brace** themselves for harsh winters. Advertisements offered leather **bridles|bridle** for horses and new oil lamps whose **brightness**, the makers claimed, would \"turn night into day\". A gas **burner** for cooking was described as a luxury that only **bourgeois** families could afford.\n\nWeddings received special attention. One report describes a young **bridegroom** arriving on horseback while a soldier played the **bugle**. Another describes a **burial** in the same church a week later, when the bride's grandfather died. Life and death, it seems, were always close together.\n\nThe bulletin also recorded scandals. In 1912, villagers organised a **boycott** of a shop after its owner was accused of offering a **bribe** to a local official. A **burglar** who stole silver from the vicar's house became the **butt** of jokes for years after he was caught asleep in a haystack. A travelling monk who gave a talk on **Buddhism** in 1925 attracted a surprisingly large crowd.\n\nIndustry slowly arrived. In the 1930s, a factory opened with a huge coal-fired **boiler**, and readers complained about smoke, an unwanted **by-product** of progress. The school began to teach **calculus**, and the bulletin proudly reported that the village scales had passed an official **calibration** test. Small details, I realised, often tell the biggest stories."},"57":{"w":["capacitance","capacitor","capsule","cartridge","catalyst","catholic","cavity","censor","ceramic","charcoal","chasm","chatter","chestnut","chorus","cigar","cigaret","circus","clamp","clatter","clearing"],"title":"The Time Capsule","t":"Last spring, workers repairing the wall of an old **catholic** school discovered a metal **capsule** hidden in a small **cavity** behind the bricks. A note on top said it had been buried by students in 1975. The school invited former pupils to watch it being opened in a forest **clearing** behind the playground.\n\nIt took two teachers and a heavy **clamp** to open the rusty lid. Inside, wrapped in newspaper, was a strange collection of objects: a **ceramic** cup painted by a child, a **charcoal** drawing of the school, a ticket to a travelling **circus** and a handful of dried **chestnuts|chestnut**. There was even a packet of **cigarets|cigaret** and an old **cigar**, which caused much laughter among the crowd.\n\nA science teacher had added parts of a radio, including a small **capacitor**. Today's physics teacher used it to explain **capacitance** to her students, and she showed them how much electronics has changed. A printer **cartridge** from the 1990s, added later by another class, looked almost as old-fashioned.\n\nThe capsule also contained letters. Some students had written about their hopes for the future, while others complained that the principal liked to **censor** articles in the school newspaper. As the letters were read aloud, the excited **chatter** of the crowd fell silent. Several former students, now in their sixties, wiped their eyes.\n\nThe event became a **catalyst** for conversation between generations. Young students were surprised to discover the **chasm** between their lives and those of their grandparents, but they also found much in common. At the end, the school **chorus** sang, and a box fell over with a loud **clatter**, making everyone laugh. The students plan to bury a new capsule next year."},"58":{"w":["climax","clockwise","clown","clutch","coefficient","coffin","coherent","collide","colonist","combustion","commentary","commonsense","compact","compatible","compatriot","composite","compression","conceit","concession","configuration"],"title":"A Day at the Racetrack","t":"Last summer, I went to my first motor race. The circuit, built on farmland first settled by a **colonist** from Scotland two centuries ago, runs **clockwise** around a lake. Thousands of fans filled the stands, and a **clown** entertained children near the entrance while families queued at every food **concession**.\n\nBefore the race, an engineer gave a tour of the garages. She explained how each car's engine uses **combustion** and high **compression** to produce enormous power. The cars are built from **composite** materials that are light but strong, and every part must be **compatible** with the rest. Even the tyres are designed with a precise **coefficient** of friction in mind. The whole **configuration**, she said, changes from track to track.\n\nSafety has improved greatly. Early racing cars were so dangerous that drivers called them \"**coffins|coffin** on wheels\". Today, there are **commonsense** rules for everything, from seat belts to fire protection. A modern car is **compact**, but its cockpit is designed to protect the driver even if two cars **collide** at high speed.\n\nThe race itself was thrilling. The radio **commentary** was so fast and excited that it was barely **coherent**. Our local hero, a young driver from our town, was competing against a **compatriot** who had won the year before. The champion's **conceit** was famous; he had told reporters he could not be beaten.\n\nThe **climax** came on the final lap. As the two cars entered the last corner, the champion's **clutch** failed, and his car slowed suddenly. Our driver passed him and crossed the line first. The crowd exploded with joy, and strangers hugged each other in the stands."},"59":{"w":["confirmation","conformity","congest","conqueror","consequent","console","conspicuous","consul","contradictory","contrived","converge","convict","correspondence","corrode","corrosion","corrupt","cosmic","costume","courteous","crank"],"title":"Letters from the Deep","t":"Off the coast of Western Australia, divers recently found the wreck of a sailing ship that sank in 1854. The ship had carried **convicts|convict** to the colony, along with supplies and mail. Among the objects recovered was a sealed metal box containing the **correspondence** of a British **consul**.\n\nGetting the box to the surface was delicate work. Salt water can **corrode** metal quickly, and **corrosion** had damaged much of the ship. Divers used an old hand-turned **crank** to raise the box slowly, and scientists dried the letters for months before opening them. **Confirmation** of the consul's identity came from a seal on the box.\n\nThe letters reveal a world of politics and intrigue. The consul describes **corrupt** officials who took money from traders, and he complains about the **conformity** expected of colonial staff. Some of his reports are **contradictory**, suggesting that he may have changed his mind, or been under pressure. His language is **courteous**, but his anger is **conspicuous**.\n\nHistorians are fascinated. Several theories about the wreck have been proposed, but some seem **contrived**. The most likely explanation is that the ship was caught in a storm as it tried to enter a harbour **congested|congest** with traffic. Its loss, and the **consequent** investigation, caused a political scandal in London.\n\nDescendants of the people on board have come to see the objects, which now **converge** in a museum exhibition alongside reproduction **costumes|costume** from the period. Curators try to **console** families who learn of their ancestors' fate. The consul once called the colony a \"tiny speck in the **cosmic** ocean\". Unlike a **conqueror** who wins land by force, he wrote, a good official should win trust."},"60":{"w":["credential","crooked","cruelty","cuckoo","curative","curly","curt","customary","cutter","cylinder","damn","datum","deafen","dean","decimal","declaration","decompose","decree","defendant","deferential"],"title":"Testing an Old Remedy","t":"For generations, villagers in a mountain region have used a bitter herbal tea to treat fevers. Many believe it has **curative** powers, and drinking it during illness is **customary**. Recently, a medical school decided to test whether the tradition has scientific value.\n\nThe project was led by the school's **dean**, a respected doctor with impressive **credentials|credential**. She organised a careful study in which every **datum** was recorded to two **decimal** places. Researchers grew the herb in the laboratory, used a sharp **cutter** to collect leaves and stored them in glass **cylinders|cylinder**. They also studied how quickly the plant would **decompose** after being picked.\n\nThe results were mixed. The tea did reduce fever slightly, but less than common medicines. Still, the dean refused to dismiss it. \"We should not **damn** traditional knowledge simply because it is old,\" she said. Her younger colleagues were **deferential**, though some privately doubted the work.\n\nThe study also became part of a legal case. A company had been selling the herb with a false **declaration** that it could cure serious diseases. The government issued a **decree** banning such claims, and the company's owner became the **defendant** in a court case. Prosecutors described him as **crooked**, while his lawyer gave **curt** answers to reporters. Animal rights groups also accused the company of **cruelty** in its testing methods.\n\nAway from the courtroom, life in the village continues. In the evenings, the **cuckoo** calls from the forest, and children with **curly** hair play outside while grandparents brew the tea. Loud music from a wedding sometimes threatens to **deafen** the whole valley. Whatever the science says, the villagers will keep drinking it."},"61":{"w":["deflect","deflection","deformation","demography","denounce","dentist","dependant","deportation","deposition","derivation","descendant","descent","desolate","despatch","detain","detective","devotion","devour","diagnostic","dictator"],"title":"The Dentist Who Became a Detective","t":"By day, Sarah Klein is a **dentist** in a busy city clinic. By night, she is a kind of **detective**, searching through old records to uncover the story of her family. Her **devotion** to the project has lasted ten years, and she now knows more about her ancestors than anyone alive.\n\nThe work began with a simple question about the **derivation** of her surname. Sarah discovered that she was a **descendant** of a farming family from Eastern Europe and that her line of **descent** could be traced back three hundred years. She learned to read old census records, where each household listed its head and every **dependant**, and she began to **devour** books on historical **demography**.\n\nSome discoveries were painful. Her great-grandparents had lived under a **dictator** in the 1930s. When neighbours chose to **denounce** them to the authorities, the family was **detained|detain** and later faced **deportation** to a **desolate** region in the north. Sarah found a government **despatch** ordering their removal, and later a **deposition** given by a survivor after the war.\n\nSarah says that her medical training helps. Like a **diagnostic** test, historical research requires careful attention to small details. Old photographs present special challenges; heat and time can cause the **deformation** of glass plates, and she has learned to repair them.\n\nHer relatives were not always supportive. When she asked difficult questions, some would **deflect** them by changing the subject. Sarah understood the **deflection**. \"Some memories are too heavy,\" she says. \"But if we forget, history can repeat itself.\""},"62":{"w":["diligence","diploma","discontented","discrete","disgrace","disillusion","disillusioned","dismay","disobedience","disorient","dispatch","disposition","disservice","dissipate","diverge","divine","dormant","dove","drainage","duke"],"title":"The Duke's Garden","t":"When Emma graduated with a **diploma** in landscape design, she hoped to work for a famous company. Instead, she found herself unemployed for six months. She became **discontented** and **disillusioned**, and she began to wonder whether her studies had been a waste of time.\n\nThen she saw an advertisement: the descendants of a **duke** wanted someone to restore his eighteenth-century garden. The garden had fallen into **disgrace** after the last gardener was dismissed for stealing, and it had been abandoned for decades. Emma applied and got the job.\n\nThe work demanded great **diligence**. The **drainage** system had collapsed, turning lawns into swamps. The garden was divided into **discrete** sections, each with its own design, and paths would **diverge** into a confusing maze that could **disorient** visitors. To her **dismay**, many old plants had died. Yet Emma found that seeds can remain **dormant** in soil for years, and when she cleared the ground, wild flowers appeared.\n\nEmma had a stubborn **disposition**. When the family's manager told her to plant modern flowers, she refused. Her **disobedience** caused tension, but she argued that modern plants would do the garden a **disservice**. Each week she would **dispatch** a report explaining her decisions, and slowly the family came to trust her.\n\nThree years later, the garden has reopened. Morning fog begins to **dissipate** over the lake, and a white **dove** sits on the old stone tower. Visitors describe the view as almost **divine**. Emma no longer feels any **disillusion**. \"Sometimes,\" she says, \"the job you didn't want is the one you were meant to do.\""},"63":{"w":["dully","dynamical","dynamo","earthenware","eastward","electrician","electrode","elegant","elemental","eligible","elliptical","eloquence","embassy","emigrate","enchant","enclosure","energize","entreat","envious","epoch"],"title":"My Grandfather the Electrician","t":"My grandfather was an **electrician**. In 1956, he decided to **emigrate** from a small village in Poland to Canada, hoping to find work and freedom. To get a visa, he queued for days outside the Canadian **embassy**, and he had to **entreat** officials to accept his papers. In the end, he was declared **eligible**, and he sailed west with one suitcase.\n\nHe began a new **epoch** of his life in a cold prairie town. His first job was wiring farms that had never had electricity. He installed power in barns and lit an animal **enclosure** so that farmers could check their cattle at night. Neighbours were **envious** of the farms he had connected, and soon he had more work than he could handle.\n\nMy grandfather loved to explain how things worked. He would take apart a bicycle **dynamo** to show me how motion makes electricity, or point to an **electrode** inside an old battery. He described the **dynamical** behaviour of currents with great **eloquence**, using his hands to draw lines in the air. Physics, he said, was about **elemental** forces, the basic powers of nature.\n\nHe admired **elegant** solutions. \"The best design is the simplest,\" he would say, sketching an **elliptical** curve on a napkin. He believed that good work could **energize** a whole community. Old lamps in his workshop glowed **dully** on winter evenings, and he stored screws in a row of **earthenware** jars.\n\nWhen he was eighty, he travelled **eastward** across the ocean one last time to visit his home village. Old friends gathered to hear his stories, and his tales seemed to **enchant** the children. \"I gave light to strangers,\" he told them. \"That is a good life.\""},"64":{"w":["equilibrium","eradicate","errand","everlasting","excrete","execution","exile","experimentally","exploitation","extraordinarily","fable","fabricate","fabrication","faction","fastener","faultless","fearless","fell","fellowship","ferrous"],"title":"Plants That Clean the Earth","t":"For centuries, the **exploitation** of mines has left scars on the landscape. Waste rock rich in **ferrous** metals and toxic chemicals pollutes soil and water, and nothing seems to grow. Now a young scientist believes that the solution may lie in plants.\n\nDr. Ana Pereira, who holds a research **fellowship** at a university in Portugal, studies plants that absorb metals through their roots. Some species can store **extraordinarily** high levels of metal in their leaves, while others **excrete** it through special cells. **Experimentally**, she has shown that planting them on old mine land can reduce pollution by half within five years.\n\nHer work aims to restore the natural **equilibrium** of damaged land, not just to **eradicate** pollution. Before planting, her team must **fell** dead trees and clear rubble, often using simple tools held together with a metal **fastener**. Ana is **fearless**, climbing into old mine shafts to collect samples. Even small tasks, such as an **errand** to buy seeds, sometimes become long adventures in remote villages.\n\nScience has its politics, too. Two years ago, a rival researcher was found to **fabricate** results, and the **fabrication** damaged the reputation of the whole field. A **faction** within her institute argued that funding should be cut. Ana's own record, however, is **faultless**, and the **execution** of her projects has impressed government inspectors.\n\nThe region has a long history. A famous poet once lived here in **exile**, writing a **fable** about a dragon that poisoned the land and a girl who healed it with flowers. Ana likes to quote it. \"Nothing is **everlasting**,\" she says, \"not even damage. The land can heal, if we help it.\""},"65":{"w":["fighter","filament","filth","finely","fireplace","firework","firmness","first-rate","fission","flake","flank","flannel","flap","flask","flatten","fling","fluorescent","flutter","flux","foil"],"title":"New Year at Grandfather's House","t":"Every New Year's Eve, our family gathers at my grandfather's old stone house in the countryside. He is ninety now and walks slowly, but his mind is sharp. In his youth, he was a **fighter** pilot, and later he worked as an engineer at a nuclear power plant, where he studied **fission**.\n\nPreparations begin in the morning. My father cleans years of soot and **filth** from the chimney so that the **fireplace** can be lit. My mother wraps fish in silver **foil** and chops vegetables **finely** for dumplings. Grandfather sits in his chair wearing a soft **flannel** shirt, drinking tea from an old **flask** and giving instructions with surprising **firmness**.\n\nGrandfather loves old technology. He keeps a collection of light bulbs, from the earliest ones with a glowing **filament** to modern LEDs. He dislikes **fluorescent** lamps, which he says give \"a cold hospital light\". When we ask about the nuclear plant, he explains how atoms split, releasing huge energy. The world, he says, is always in **flux**.\n\nAs evening falls, snow begins to drift down in large **flakes|flake**. Flags **flutter** on the roof, and the wind makes the door **flap** open and shut. Children **fling** snowballs at each other, then run inside to **flatten** their wet clothes in front of the fire. Tall pine trees **flank** the path to the house like guards.\n\nAt midnight, we go outside to watch the **firework** display over the village. Grandfather stands at the door, wrapped in a blanket. \"A **first-rate** show,\" he says, smiling. \"But the best part is having you all here.\""}});
